WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:06.300
Welcome to the MIT Center for
Art, Science &amp; Technology's

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.190
2021 Symposium
"Unfolding Intelligence:

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:14.690
the Art and Science of
Contemporary Computation".

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:16.950
CAST was established
in 2012 with the goal

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.400
of building and
building on connections

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.310
between the worlds of art,
science, and technology.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:24.130
This is the third in
a series of symposia

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:26.040
that CAST has
convened since then.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.760
And as with its predecessors,
we bring together

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:33.120
artists, scientists, engineers,
and humanists from within MIT

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.590
and from the world
at large to discuss

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:37.640
areas of
rapidly-evolving research

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:39.039
and urgent social relevance.

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:43.329
And to find, in that dialogue,
stimulation, confirmation,

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:48.750
provocation, intersection,
and, we hope, common purpose.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.690
At MIT, CAST partners with
departments, labs, and centers

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:55.140
to integrate the arts
across the curriculum,

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.560
to enrich and encourage artistic
collaboration, and to provide

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:01.789
support to faculty and
members of the MIT community

00:01:01.789 --> 00:01:03.820
as they pursue their
own artistic practice

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.970
and/or research.

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:09.360
In addition to
symposia like this,

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.440
CAST facilitates the sharing
of this creative work

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.320
beyond the Institute
by producing concerts,

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.590
exhibitions, and
publications, and making

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:19.380
them available to the public.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:21.990
So thank you for
being with us today.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:23.950
We hope you will join
us throughout the week

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:27.560
at virtual events addressing
the aesthetic, technical,

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:30.700
and critical issues pertaining
to artificial intelligence

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:33.130
and computational media.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.700
We also look forward to
seeing you on Friday,

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.430
April 9th as the
symposium culminates

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.909
with a live interactive
event to which

00:01:40.909 --> 00:01:42.430
all attendees are invited.

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:45.299
And which you can join
presenters and artists

00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:48.780
in breakout rooms to explore
hidden threads between all that

00:01:48.780 --> 00:02:53.560
has been discussed this week.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:55.951
Welcome to Unfolding
Intelligence, the Art

00:02:55.951 --> 00:02:58.490
and Science of
Contemporary Computation.

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:01.170
Thank you for joining us and
for bringing your curiosity

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.340
and attention with you today.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:08.510
Deep Time and Intelligence,
Unfolding Models, Open Systems,

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:10.020
Bias in AI--

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:11.450
even in this sheer,
titular variety

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:15.280
I hope you might hear whispers
of the ambitions afoot.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.520
Could the discussion of
artificial intelligence

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.990
be reorganized by
the thoughts and work

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:23.570
of those who question AI, who
cautiously embrace and reshape

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:27.370
it to their needs and from
particular perspectives?

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:30.420
Those who work with and through
it, grounding our ideas about

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:33.060
and response to AI,
in such practices

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:37.370
that operate within the
orbit of these situated bases

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:39.060
of reflection.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.340
Perhaps then, rather
than being strung along

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:45.290
by stories about inevitable
outcomes, impacts, and profits,

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:47.480
singularities, et
cetera, all reasoning

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.790
that assumes the essence
of AI is a sure thing,

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:53.470
that its effect on us and
our role in shaping it

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:56.660
are readily apprehensible
relations with a stable thing,

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:58.960
a predictable future.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:03.150
Relieving ourselves of
that overexposure of AI

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.720
to intellectual
sureties, we may sense

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:09.380
a moment in which an instrument
of optimization and automation

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:13.340
becomes a medium open to
reuse and comment, tinkering,

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:17.889
and informed reflection, even a
generator of surprise results.

00:04:17.889 --> 00:04:20.130
Deep Time and
Intelligence, the portion

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:21.910
of the discussion at hand.

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:23.950
In this panel, we
will see that AI

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:26.870
can help us to consider
fundamental questions about how

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:30.810
sensations, sounds, movements,
choreographies of form

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:34.260
and matter, take shape in
time, gain coherence as objects

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:37.530
and structures through training
and learning, recursion,

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:38.790
and iteration.

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:41.691
Deep time, yes, in terms
of the evolutionary time

00:04:41.691 --> 00:04:43.850
scales at work in
growth and form

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:47.940
from the diatom to the social,
but also deep, as in downward,

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:51.511
to the nano and minute intervals
of the fold of a protein

00:04:51.511 --> 00:04:53.720
or a crack in a crystal lattice.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:55.750
AI seems to assist
us in traveling

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:58.760
to scales at which primordial,
logical formalisms operate

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:01.460
in dimensions and
through structures that

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:04.060
defy our concepts
and intuitions,

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:07.160
but perhaps not those
of feature detection

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:12.000
capacities exacting
computations can process.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.750
Holly Herndon,
artist-composer-performer,

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:19.320
has built and conceptualized
for us a way of thinking music

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:23.990
in the context of a longue
durée of ritualized entertainment.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:26.500
Gary Tomlinson,
Yale musicologist,

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:28.730
provides connections between
the story of evolution

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:32.150
and the unfolding of AI research
that few could account for,

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:34.490
given the myriad methods and
fields of research that he

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:36.890
has been able to synthesize,
building connections

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:40.420
between humanities
and sciences for us.

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:44.000
Markus Buehler, material
scientist here at MIT,

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.550
challenges us to think
of AI as something

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:47.940
capable of detecting
homologies of structure

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:51.020
across scales of existence,
perhaps even giving us

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:55.090
new ways of thinking about
universals across scales.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:57.140
Antonio Torralba,
computer scientist

00:05:57.140 --> 00:06:01.150
with CSAIL here at MIT,
moves not between scales

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:03.300
but across modes
of sensation, using

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.810
AI to link sound to vision
and movement to sound,

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:09.780
proposing a multimodal
sensory field for AI.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.960
I shall withhold
any further attempt

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.980
at synthetic remarks at
present and listen now with you

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:18.060
all to the thoughts of
this wonderful group,

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.520
inviting you to listen with
me for hints of how AI might

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:24.330
be providing new insight
into form and structure,

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:27.450
cohering at divergent
length scales, time scales,

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:29.200
and between modalities
of sensation

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:32.370
and varying protocols
of entrainment.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:35.710
It is important also now to
acknowledge that it is not

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:38.480
we the speakers nor MIT
who claim authority over

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:43.370
or seek final say regarding the
deep or any ultimate meaning

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:44.990
of ancient ancestry.

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:47.490
MIT acknowledges
Indigenous peoples

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:49.670
as the traditional
stewards of the land

00:06:49.670 --> 00:06:51.080
and the enduring
relationship that

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:53.870
exists between them and their
traditional territories.

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:56.430
The land over which
circuits sustaining

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:58.669
MIT send their signals
to your screens

00:06:58.669 --> 00:07:01.110
is the traditional
unceded territory

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:03.360
of the Wampanoag Nation.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:05.270
We offer our
partial perspectives

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:08.460
in acknowledging that
we have much to learn,

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:10.090
and that an acknowledgment
such as this

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:12.919
is only a small first step
towards remembering and working

00:07:12.919 --> 00:07:15.260
through the painful
histories of genocide

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:21.900
and forced occupation that
the Wampanoag Nation suffered,

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:24.840
land on which many diverse
Indigenous groups gathered

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:26.780
since time immemorial.

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:28.460
My name is William Lockett.

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:30.139
I'm the current
Mellon postdoctoral

00:07:30.139 --> 00:07:33.250
fellow with the Center for
Art, Science, and Technology.

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:35.720
You'll see me at the Q&amp;A,
leading the discussion

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:38.320
and also taking your
questions through Twitter.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:40.760
Thank you and see you then.

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:44.960
Good morning,
afternoon, or evening.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:46.040
Thank you for watching.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:47.840
I'm Gary Tomlinson.

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:50.020
Provocative titles are
meant not merely to provoke,

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:51.560
but to draw us in.

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:52.860
And one of the more
compelling instances

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:56.300
I've come across recently is
this one from a 2020 article

00:07:56.300 --> 00:08:01.180
by J. Mark Bishop, "Artificial
Intelligence is Stupid

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:06.510
and Causal Reasoning
Won't Fix It."

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:08.590
The essay opposes
computationalism,

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:11.500
the idea that human intelligence
is a strictly computational

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:14.290
affair, and it argues
that AI, since it

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:17.470
is a computational affair,
can't duplicate the feats

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:20.410
of human-like understanding.

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:22.540
Bishop's article is one
in a long line opposed

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:25.960
to computationalism, both as
an approach to the human brain

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:29.150
and as a way of making a
machine that might act like it.

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:31.830
And he takes us on a quick tour
of some of his predecessors,

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:33.750
from Godel's
incompleteness theorem,

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:36.580
as developed by John
Lucas and Roger Penrose,

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:38.760
to John Searle's
Chinese room, riffing

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:40.510
on the distinction
of formal syntax

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:44.190
from semantics, then building
on Hilary Putnam's idea

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:48.610
that all open systems are, in
effect, finite state automata.

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:52.560
Bishop reasons that equating
mind with computation leads

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:56.620
inevitably to pan-psychism, to
the assignment of mind to every

00:08:56.620 --> 00:09:01.520
open system--to a
rock, for example.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:03.800
What worries me about
much writing of this sort

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:05.540
is its proliferation
of key words

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:09.790
that are rarely defined as
clearly as they need to be.

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:11.650
Bishop's review runs
through a litany of them.

00:09:11.650 --> 00:09:14.720
It's true that he mostly
avoids the vaguest terms, mind

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:17.510
and consciousness, but here
are some difficult words

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:19.690
he does rely on that
call out for unpacking

00:09:19.690 --> 00:09:21.450
but receive little.

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:24.390
Understanding, as in the
human-like understanding

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:26.029
that automata don't achieve.

00:09:26.029 --> 00:09:28.330
Or meaning, as in
the meaning of words

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:31.040
computational
machines can't grasp.

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:34.610
Other keywords that pop up
are synonyms for meaning.

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:36.440
Semantics and
content both appear

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:39.700
in Bishop's discussion of
Searle's Chinese room argument.

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:43.740
Two more difficult keywords
are experience and salience.

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:47.140
Is salience dependent on
experience or a part of it

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:48.800
or a generator of it?

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:52.160
What is experience, and how
does salience come about?

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:56.030
In this lexical thicket,
meaning is, for me,

00:09:56.030 --> 00:09:57.861
the keyword of
keywords, and we can

00:09:57.861 --> 00:10:00.060
expand the list of
closely related terms

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:03.610
beyond Searle's
content and semantics.

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:08.040
Reference, representation,
aboutness, intentionality, just

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:12.130
to give four examples.

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:14.370
In my remarks today,
I want to make

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:17.870
two observations about meaning
and its synonym cluster,

00:10:17.870 --> 00:10:20.350
observations that seem to be
relevant to the possibilities

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:24.170
of a Strong AI or an
AGI, Artificial General

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:25.170
Intelligence.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:27.440
The first observation
concerns the foundation

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:29.150
of meaning, which I
locate in semiosis,

00:10:29.150 --> 00:10:31.649
that is, in sign making.

00:10:31.649 --> 00:10:33.770
The theory I draw
on here is generally

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:36.870
humanistic in orientation.

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:39.870
The second observation
concerns the origins and extent

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:41.990
of the meaning that
arises from semiosis,

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:45.650
and here, my theoretical base
is in evolutionary science.

00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:47.720
To look ahead,
these observations

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:50.250
will lead me toward a couple
of general caveats regarding

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:51.690
machine intelligence.

00:10:51.690 --> 00:10:54.330
First, in order to see the
challenges of designing

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:57.220
a machine capable of
understanding meanings,

00:10:57.220 --> 00:11:00.330
we need a firm grasp of the
nature of meaning itself.

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:01.810
This requires a
distinction that I

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:04.980
regard as fundamental and
ontological between information

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:08.240
transmission, a basic
process to all life forms,

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:11.850
and sign making, a particular
kind of information

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:14.440
transmission restricted
to a very small proportion

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:17.160
of those life forms.

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:18.660
Information transmission
of the first sort,

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:20.910
no matter how
powerful or elaborate,

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:23.050
can't lead to meaning,
content, or aboutness

00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:25.550
because it can't create signs.

00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:27.710
Only a machine capable
of semiotic information

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:30.790
could create or
understand meaning.

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:34.790
Second, once we see the
distinction between information

00:11:34.790 --> 00:11:37.670
in general and the special
case of semiotic information,

00:11:37.670 --> 00:11:39.920
we discern more clearly
the evolved capacities

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:42.910
that enable the creation of
science and hence, meaning.

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:46.130
These are not identifiable
traits possessed

00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:49.610
by particular organisms,
not adaptations selected

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:51.880
for particular functions.

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:55.500
Such selection-for is not
how natural selection works.

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:57.529
It's not an
optimization problem,

00:11:57.529 --> 00:12:00.440
notwithstanding Dan
Dennett's decades-long effort

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:02.330
to make us think it is.

00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:04.800
The capacities to create
meaning are instead

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:07.970
ongoing processes of negotiation
with an external world,

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:11.529
and so they can't be identified
as discrete properties

00:12:11.529 --> 00:12:13.170
of an organism.

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:15.260
Processes, not properties.

00:12:15.260 --> 00:12:16.871
This is a motto that
should hang above the door

00:12:16.871 --> 00:12:18.529
of every evolutionist.

00:12:18.529 --> 00:12:21.320
It already hangs above
the doors of many.

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:24.190
These processes are signaled
in the "radical niche

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:26.480
construction" of my title.

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:28.839
OK, so much for looking ahead.

00:12:28.839 --> 00:12:31.690
Let's start with the distinction
of non-semiotic and semiotic

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:32.840
information.

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:36.130
The first could be called
information, pure and simple,

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:40.130
or Shannon information, but
I'll call it causal information,

00:12:40.130 --> 00:12:42.110
since it's a matter
of causal covariance,

00:12:42.110 --> 00:12:45.050
as Jerry Fodor once put it.

00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:47.110
Its transmission along
some kind of channel

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:51.430
brings about some corresponding
effect at the receiving end.

00:12:51.430 --> 00:12:53.620
Information of this sort
is a relational thing.

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:56.350
The channels can feed back
from receiver to sender.

00:12:56.350 --> 00:12:59.850
They can be networked in
parallel and serial fashion,

00:12:59.850 --> 00:13:02.850
doubled and redoubled again,
and hierarchized in any number

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:04.339
of routines and subroutines.

00:13:04.339 --> 00:13:07.170
But the fundamental
relationality of covariance

00:13:07.170 --> 00:13:09.250
remains.

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:11.630
All living things, even
the simplest bacterium,

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:15.770
are complex miracles of this
kind of information processing,

00:13:15.770 --> 00:13:18.300
but their information in the
vast majority of organisms

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:19.990
is not about anything.

00:13:19.990 --> 00:13:23.740
It's without content or
meaning, a sheer causality that

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:27.300
makes things happen from the
molecular level on up in scale

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:29.860
through organismal and
ecosystemic levels.

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:31.620
It brings about
correlated difference

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:36.730
in the complex systems that
work at all these levels.

00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:38.279
Semiotic information
is something different

00:13:38.279 --> 00:13:41.410
that arises on the foundation
of causal information.

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:43.209
It depends not on
systems of relations

00:13:43.209 --> 00:13:48.140
alone, but on a relation to
a regulation, a metarelation.

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:50.970
This requires something more
complex than the Shannon

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:53.390
model of transmitter
channel and receiver

00:13:53.390 --> 00:13:55.350
and something different
even from the idea

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:57.570
of a hierarchy of routines.

00:13:57.570 --> 00:13:59.260
It requires
something so complex,

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:00.850
in fact, that it
has emerged only

00:14:00.850 --> 00:14:06.089
in a tiny percentage of the life
forms our biosphere has known.

00:14:06.089 --> 00:14:07.580
While all life
forms are processors

00:14:07.580 --> 00:14:10.230
of causal information,
very few are creators

00:14:10.230 --> 00:14:12.250
of semiotic information.

00:14:12.250 --> 00:14:16.630
This basic distinction of
causal and semiotic information

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:18.190
is relevant to the
question of meaning

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:22.580
because meaning is founded
in the sign metarelation.

00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:24.209
Signs are always signs of.

00:14:24.209 --> 00:14:26.010
They introduce
aboutness in the world

00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:28.540
by virtue of this fundamental
nature, introducing

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:31.930
at the same time what we connote
by all the synonyms named

00:14:31.930 --> 00:14:35.430
above: content,
semantics, and so forth.

00:14:35.430 --> 00:14:38.220
Signs are representational
in the exact sense

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:40.959
of re-presenting one
thing in another.

00:14:40.959 --> 00:14:42.310
This is what signs do.

00:14:42.310 --> 00:14:44.589
It's not what causal
information does.

00:14:44.589 --> 00:14:48.130
Signs are not the same
as signals in the way

00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:49.240
this word is loosely but
pervasively used by biologists.

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:53.640
A signal is non-semiotic
and usually comes

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:55.899
down to a threshold
mechanism in the operation

00:14:55.899 --> 00:14:57.300
of causal information.

00:14:57.300 --> 00:15:01.800
It doesn't re-present
one thing in another.

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:04.680
A metarelation is required
for semiosis most basically

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:07.061
because a sign is something
different than a cause

00:15:07.061 --> 00:15:08.061
of its object.

00:15:08.061 --> 00:15:11.200
It is instead an
analysis of its object.

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:13.480
It divides up the
object, picking out

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:18.480
certain aspects of it to
use in re-presenting it,

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:21.050
its complexity
constrained in the picking

00:15:21.050 --> 00:15:25.080
both by its own nature and
by the nature of the object.

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:27.540
Signs and their objects
never mesh completely.

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:29.870
That would collapse the
sign into the object,

00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:32.850
collapsing at the same
time aboutness and content.

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:34.920
There's no re-presenting
in identity.

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:39.950
But what is it that enters
into this complex relation

00:15:39.950 --> 00:15:43.730
between aspects of one entity
and aspects of another?

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:46.180
What is the added something
that creates the metarelation?

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:49.579
This is a third entity,
one capable of forming

00:15:49.579 --> 00:15:51.790
percepts of certain sorts.

00:15:51.790 --> 00:15:55.080
It doesn't exercise sovereign
control over these percepts.

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:58.089
Instead, it's called into
the aspectual constraints

00:15:58.089 --> 00:16:01.589
of what will become sign and
object, called by their nature

00:16:01.589 --> 00:16:04.490
as affordances in the world.

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:08.490
Its percepts are always formed
not only by its own capacities,

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:12.420
but also by the entities that
come to form sign and object.

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:13.839
Semiosis is, in
this way, a process

00:16:13.839 --> 00:16:16.649
of reciprocal constraint,
reciprocal affordance,

00:16:16.649 --> 00:16:20.670
if you'd like, among a percept
and things or phenomena

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:22.399
external to it.

00:16:22.399 --> 00:16:25.899
It's a niche-constructive
process.

00:16:25.899 --> 00:16:29.670
Those of you familiar with the
philosophy of Charles Sanders

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:32.089
Peirce will recognize
in this approach

00:16:32.089 --> 00:16:35.590
to semiotics two
basic Peircan tenets.

00:16:35.590 --> 00:16:38.890
The first is the
partial aspectual nature

00:16:38.890 --> 00:16:42.110
of all the relations in
the sign making process.

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:44.029
From his scrutiny
of this partiality,

00:16:44.029 --> 00:16:46.720
viewed in its many
combinatorial possibilities,

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:51.320
sprang the many typologies of
science that Peirce offered.

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:53.750
The second tenet is the
calling of a perceiver

00:16:53.750 --> 00:16:56.320
into the aspectual
parsing of the world,

00:16:56.320 --> 00:17:00.070
an idea for which Perice
coined the term interpretant.

00:17:00.070 --> 00:17:03.680
This is a fundamental term
in his philosophy but one

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:04.970
that has been much
misunderstood.

00:17:04.970 --> 00:17:08.970
It involves no interpretation,
as we tend to use that word,

00:17:08.970 --> 00:17:11.579
but relies instead on
the perceptual capacity

00:17:11.579 --> 00:17:15.140
to divide up the world,
to parse it, as I've said,

00:17:15.140 --> 00:17:18.179
registering some of its parts,
clumping some things together

00:17:18.179 --> 00:17:22.209
with others and relating
itself to those relations.

00:17:22.209 --> 00:17:27.230
How do some life forms come to
be able to form interpretants?

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:29.999
Memory is crucial,
but not merely

00:17:29.999 --> 00:17:32.659
the simplest forms of
storage and retrieval.

00:17:32.659 --> 00:17:35.499
Instead, something like an
episodic memory is necessary,

00:17:35.499 --> 00:17:37.230
a mechanism that can
retrieve memories

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:40.169
as parts of whole
situations, retrieve them

00:17:40.169 --> 00:17:42.580
more readily because
of those situations

00:17:42.580 --> 00:17:46.870
and enrich and relive the
situations in the retrieval.

00:17:46.870 --> 00:17:48.499
Some animals besides
humans are capable

00:17:48.499 --> 00:17:49.799
of such episodic memory.

00:17:49.799 --> 00:17:54.190
Most animals, and all
other organisms, are not.

00:17:54.190 --> 00:17:57.590
Learning is also central, but
learning of an advanced sort

00:17:57.590 --> 00:18:00.620
dependent on episodic memory.

00:18:00.620 --> 00:18:03.139
Simple associative learning
is a purely relational thing

00:18:03.139 --> 00:18:04.889
and occurs in many,
many creatures

00:18:04.889 --> 00:18:08.830
without any semiosis as a
function of simple neural nets

00:18:08.830 --> 00:18:11.259
involving excitation
and inhibition connected

00:18:11.259 --> 00:18:14.570
with the innate reward
or punishment signals.

00:18:14.570 --> 00:18:18.460
A well-studied example is
the sucrose sensitivity

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:20.500
of honeybees on the
basis of which they

00:18:20.500 --> 00:18:22.519
learn complex
associations concerning

00:18:22.519 --> 00:18:25.190
food sources and much else.

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:27.600
This is non-semiotic learning.

00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:30.480
On the other hand, the
learning of a songbird,

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:33.020
for example, to rank
the varied challenges

00:18:33.020 --> 00:18:35.739
from the songs of its
neighbors or to sing by itself

00:18:35.739 --> 00:18:38.010
a pseudo-duet suggesting
to other birds

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:40.460
that its mate is
present when it's not,

00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:43.270
these are semiotic
through and through.

00:18:43.270 --> 00:18:45.190
They depend in each case on
the retrieval and processing

00:18:45.190 --> 00:18:47.710
of whole episodes
from a bird's life.

00:18:47.710 --> 00:18:49.419
And from these,
they parse aspects

00:18:49.419 --> 00:18:52.130
that conduce to
interpretant formation.

00:18:52.130 --> 00:18:56.330
Memory, learning, and
finally attention.

00:18:56.330 --> 00:18:58.159
Attention is a
more basic capacity

00:18:58.159 --> 00:18:59.519
than memory or learning.

00:18:59.519 --> 00:19:03.269
And it too comes in more
and less complex forms.

00:19:03.269 --> 00:19:05.279
It's related even in
its simplest forms

00:19:05.279 --> 00:19:07.970
to the salience I mentioned
before, the way an organism

00:19:07.970 --> 00:19:10.529
can single out a particular
stimulus from all those

00:19:10.529 --> 00:19:11.909
coming to it.

00:19:11.909 --> 00:19:14.380
The sensitivity of
honeybees to sucrose again

00:19:14.380 --> 00:19:16.440
provides a good example.

00:19:16.440 --> 00:19:20.129
For semiosis, a more
complex attentional capacity

00:19:20.129 --> 00:19:23.509
is required, one that can
sustain a recursive analysis

00:19:23.509 --> 00:19:27.059
of incoming stimuli, taking
them apart into their aspects

00:19:27.059 --> 00:19:29.830
and focusing on them at both
comprehensive and partial

00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:30.830
levels.

00:19:30.830 --> 00:19:34.190
In this analytic or
parsing function,

00:19:34.190 --> 00:19:35.330
attention enables
the construction

00:19:35.330 --> 00:19:37.270
of episodes in
memory and learning,

00:19:37.270 --> 00:19:41.350
building memories as aspects
of broader situations.

00:19:41.350 --> 00:19:42.730
Studies of such
complex attention

00:19:42.730 --> 00:19:46.350
suggests that it's the product
of mediations of information

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:50.049
in which salience filters
proximate to sensory input

00:19:50.049 --> 00:19:52.710
interface with further
processing and modulation

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:53.710
downstream.

00:19:53.710 --> 00:19:56.159
Interface, that is,
with top-down systems

00:19:56.159 --> 00:19:58.559
higher up in the
midbrain and forebrain.

00:19:58.559 --> 00:20:01.649
Such neural mediations
are, I think, fundamental

00:20:01.649 --> 00:20:03.279
to the emergence of
semiotic information,

00:20:03.279 --> 00:20:07.019
and I'll touch on
them again later.

00:20:07.019 --> 00:20:11.269
Attention, memory, learning,
these foundations for semiosis

00:20:11.269 --> 00:20:14.039
are not, as I said before,
discrete properties

00:20:14.039 --> 00:20:17.399
of certain animals, but instead
processes in their engagements

00:20:17.399 --> 00:20:19.320
with their niches.

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:22.220
Recognizing the flexible
complexity of these processes

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:26.429
is what has recently
radicalized the ideas of niche

00:20:26.429 --> 00:20:28.799
construction.

00:20:28.799 --> 00:20:30.309
Let's step back for a moment.

00:20:30.309 --> 00:20:31.919
In general, niche
construction refers

00:20:31.919 --> 00:20:34.940
to the reciprocal or feedback
process by which organisms

00:20:34.940 --> 00:20:36.820
build their selective
environments

00:20:36.820 --> 00:20:39.220
as those environments
build them.

00:20:39.220 --> 00:20:41.100
This construction has
tended to be thought

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:45.649
of as a dynamic operating
across long timescales

00:20:45.649 --> 00:20:47.840
with environments and
populations of organisms

00:20:47.840 --> 00:20:50.529
slowly shifting in
tandem with one another.

00:20:50.529 --> 00:20:53.239
The ancestors of
beavers, for example,

00:20:53.239 --> 00:20:55.139
gradually adapting
to aquatic life

00:20:55.139 --> 00:20:58.030
even as their behavior has
created more and more aquatic

00:20:58.030 --> 00:21:02.759
environments.

00:21:02.759 --> 00:21:06.009
Alterations in the niche
brought about by organisms

00:21:06.009 --> 00:21:08.360
bring about shifting
selective dynamics, which

00:21:08.360 --> 00:21:11.840
eventually alter the
genomes of those organisms.

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:15.479
Now, what I'm
terming radical niche

00:21:15.479 --> 00:21:17.429
construction
involves recognizing

00:21:17.429 --> 00:21:20.590
the pervasive plasticity of
niche constructive feedback

00:21:20.590 --> 00:21:24.059
across all biotic scales
of time and space.

00:21:24.059 --> 00:21:25.320
We now see that
niche construction

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:27.629
is not only a long-term
process but also

00:21:27.629 --> 00:21:30.950
the plastic responsiveness of
all organisms to their changing

00:21:30.950 --> 00:21:35.129
environments operating
at a much quicker pace.

00:21:35.129 --> 00:21:40.210
Natural selection has resulted
not in fixed capacities

00:21:40.210 --> 00:21:42.259
with discrete
advantageous functions

00:21:42.259 --> 00:21:45.379
so much as in the flexibility
by which the nodal systems that

00:21:45.379 --> 00:21:48.539
make up organisms open
out to myriad aspects

00:21:48.539 --> 00:21:49.539
of their environments.

00:21:49.539 --> 00:21:52.840
And, this is most
important, this plasticity

00:21:52.840 --> 00:21:54.590
reaches from the
niche all the way

00:21:54.590 --> 00:21:57.669
to genes, which turn out to
be nothing like blueprints

00:21:57.669 --> 00:22:01.139
for life forms, but instead
are open information systems

00:22:01.139 --> 00:22:04.649
in constant flux with the
situations around them.

00:22:04.649 --> 00:22:07.630
An example can come once
again from honeybees.

00:22:07.630 --> 00:22:11.330
Dozens of pheromones are crucial
in the intricate regulation

00:22:11.330 --> 00:22:13.419
of the honeybee colony
or superorganism.

00:22:13.419 --> 00:22:15.989
They're produced of
course by individual bees,

00:22:15.989 --> 00:22:18.820
according to complex
bioinformational pathways

00:22:18.820 --> 00:22:23.239
involving genes, RNA
transcription of various sorts,

00:22:23.239 --> 00:22:26.549
proteins that genes
construct, and more.

00:22:26.549 --> 00:22:27.729
But the pathways run
in both directions.

00:22:27.729 --> 00:22:30.779
The genetic instructions
are regulated

00:22:30.779 --> 00:22:33.940
by the proteins they produce,
which in turn are regulated

00:22:33.940 --> 00:22:36.169
by the external circulation
of the pheromones

00:22:36.169 --> 00:22:38.169
they help to produce.

00:22:38.169 --> 00:22:40.260
Brief exposure to
a single chemical

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:43.299
in the cocktail that functions
as an alarm pheromone

00:22:43.299 --> 00:22:45.890
alters factors inducing
RNA transcription

00:22:45.890 --> 00:22:48.259
in the olfactory centers
of the bee brain,

00:22:48.259 --> 00:22:50.320
allowing genes to
be expressed that

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:52.330
activate fight or
flight and aggression

00:22:52.330 --> 00:22:54.609
pathways in higher
brain regions, which

00:22:54.609 --> 00:22:56.880
then modulate bee behavior.

00:22:56.880 --> 00:23:01.350
An hour after such exposure, the
resulting information cascades

00:23:01.350 --> 00:23:03.360
have up- or down-regulated
the expression

00:23:03.360 --> 00:23:06.020
of hundreds of genes,
laying a foundation

00:23:06.020 --> 00:23:08.799
for heightened responsiveness
to subsequent alarms.

00:23:08.799 --> 00:23:11.730
The whole network, extending
from niche to genes,

00:23:11.730 --> 00:23:16.639
has been altered in the blink of
an eye or the whiff of a scent.

00:23:16.639 --> 00:23:18.730
Similar cascades
regulate, it is now clear,

00:23:18.730 --> 00:23:23.950
the genomic expression
of all life forms.

00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:25.889
With such radical niche
construction in mind,

00:23:25.889 --> 00:23:27.880
let's return to
semiosis and ask where

00:23:27.880 --> 00:23:30.470
we might seek neural
substrates for the interpretant

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:32.039
and the metarelation.

00:23:32.039 --> 00:23:34.289
I think we need to
seek them not simply

00:23:34.289 --> 00:23:37.610
in the redoubling of parallel
processing in large brains,

00:23:37.610 --> 00:23:40.049
and not even only in
the serial extension

00:23:40.049 --> 00:23:41.940
in such brains of
processing along

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:44.460
a sequence of distinct
modules, though both of these

00:23:44.460 --> 00:23:46.349
will probably play a role.

00:23:46.349 --> 00:23:49.869
An additional element
of neural architecture

00:23:49.869 --> 00:23:53.119
is built on top of these,
bringing massive parallelism

00:23:53.119 --> 00:23:56.299
and serialism to bear in
modulating the processing

00:23:56.299 --> 00:23:58.730
of other connected centers.

00:23:58.730 --> 00:24:01.370
Instead of peripheral sensory
neurons connecting directly

00:24:01.370 --> 00:24:03.649
to the highest
processing centers,

00:24:03.649 --> 00:24:05.609
large vertebrate
brains have processors

00:24:05.609 --> 00:24:08.419
connected to processors,
all reconnected

00:24:08.419 --> 00:24:12.480
in loops of feedback systems.

00:24:12.480 --> 00:24:16.499
The song system in a songbird's
brain provides an example.

00:24:16.499 --> 00:24:19.859
This is often divided
into three systems, two

00:24:19.859 --> 00:24:24.379
pathways controlling song
learning, song learning

00:24:24.379 --> 00:24:26.519
memorization, and production,
and a third pathway

00:24:26.519 --> 00:24:30.649
for auditory production,
auditory processing.

00:24:30.649 --> 00:24:32.990
The slide I'm showing
you divides these

00:24:32.990 --> 00:24:35.289
into two sagittal
sections of the brain

00:24:35.289 --> 00:24:37.389
with the vocal
pathways on the left

00:24:37.389 --> 00:24:39.610
and the auditory
pathway on the right.

00:24:39.610 --> 00:24:43.049
It's easy to see that
the systems are networked

00:24:43.049 --> 00:24:46.480
in immensely complex ways
involving many nuclei, each

00:24:46.480 --> 00:24:48.749
with large numbers of neurons.

00:24:48.749 --> 00:24:52.110
The auditory nuclei are linked
to the song control ones,

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:54.929
thinking left- and right-hand
diagrams, in other words,

00:24:54.929 --> 00:24:59.009
and bringing audition directly
to bear on memory and learning.

00:24:59.009 --> 00:25:01.679
Especially important for
the semiotic complexity

00:25:01.679 --> 00:25:04.450
of birdsong, I think,
is the mediating role

00:25:04.450 --> 00:25:06.989
of the thalamus in
these networks, situated

00:25:06.989 --> 00:25:10.309
between mid- and hindbrain,
and higher cognitive functions.

00:25:10.309 --> 00:25:13.399
Notice that it
doesn't merely relay

00:25:13.399 --> 00:25:16.019
processed sensory input
to higher brain regions

00:25:16.019 --> 00:25:18.460
but is a part of loops
from those regions

00:25:18.460 --> 00:25:22.509
involved in both song
production and audition.

00:25:22.509 --> 00:25:26.349
In mammals, the thalamus is a
midbrain processing station,

00:25:26.349 --> 00:25:28.099
mediating top-down and
bottom-up networks.

00:25:28.099 --> 00:25:32.200
It's implicated in attentional
focus, voluntary motor

00:25:32.200 --> 00:25:34.840
behavior, memory, and
learning, and so it

00:25:34.840 --> 00:25:39.759
seems to be in songbirds.

00:25:39.759 --> 00:25:46.059
There's nothing nearly as
elaborated as these networks

00:25:46.059 --> 00:25:48.470
in the brain of a honeybee.

00:25:48.470 --> 00:25:51.269
Here, by contrast, is a
diagram of the network

00:25:51.269 --> 00:25:54.320
for sucrose sensitivity
and responses

00:25:54.320 --> 00:25:57.009
that I mentioned before.

00:25:57.009 --> 00:25:58.360
The relevant part
of this diagram

00:25:58.360 --> 00:26:01.690
is the red arrow tracing
a single long neuron that

00:26:01.690 --> 00:26:04.870
projects from the proboscis
through the antennal lobes

00:26:04.870 --> 00:26:07.710
to higher brain regions, the
so-called mushroom bodies,

00:26:07.710 --> 00:26:10.960
and to the green
motor control center.

00:26:10.960 --> 00:26:14.570
The complexity built into this
tiny brain is truly awesome,

00:26:14.570 --> 00:26:17.649
but it's far outstripped by
the bird brain's multiplicity

00:26:17.649 --> 00:26:20.840
of distinct modules,
their serial and parallel

00:26:20.840 --> 00:26:23.690
connections, and their
intricate feedback mediation

00:26:23.690 --> 00:26:27.139
through distinct
levels of processing.

00:26:27.139 --> 00:26:30.539
It's not merely the complexity
of neural architecture,

00:26:30.539 --> 00:26:33.090
however, that brings about
the cognitive functions

00:26:33.090 --> 00:26:35.549
of interpretant
sign and meaning.

00:26:35.549 --> 00:26:38.220
To achieve this, we need to
set this network in motion

00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:40.479
as a process rather
than an architecture.

00:26:40.479 --> 00:26:42.529
And the motion
comes about through

00:26:42.529 --> 00:26:44.159
the radical
niche-constructed plasticity

00:26:44.159 --> 00:26:46.739
in the face of
changing affordances

00:26:46.739 --> 00:26:49.340
I spoke about before.

00:26:49.340 --> 00:26:53.159
The informational complexities
I exemplified with the alarm

00:26:53.159 --> 00:26:55.580
pheromones of bees
altering gene expression

00:26:55.580 --> 00:26:59.440
on a moment-to-moment basis are
at work in all animal brains

00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:01.700
and extensively
studied in songbirds.

00:27:01.700 --> 00:27:05.119
They seem to foster a varied and
complex processing of the world

00:27:05.119 --> 00:27:07.820
proportionate to the varied
complexity of the brain

00:27:07.820 --> 00:27:09.529
architectures they enliven.

00:27:09.529 --> 00:27:12.830
It's from this, I
think, that a new fold

00:27:12.830 --> 00:27:15.760
is introduced into the
experience of certain animals.

00:27:15.760 --> 00:27:18.190
From this, that they
gain the parsing power

00:27:18.190 --> 00:27:21.070
that makes their percepts
analytic and at the same time

00:27:21.070 --> 00:27:23.249
opens those percepts to
the formative rebound

00:27:23.249 --> 00:27:25.440
from the things they
analyze, the calling

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:26.539
into the interpretant.

00:27:26.539 --> 00:27:29.390
From this fold, this
recursive percept,

00:27:29.390 --> 00:27:32.739
arises the cognizance of
a relation in perception

00:27:32.739 --> 00:27:36.049
to a relation of things in the
world, the essence of the sign

00:27:36.049 --> 00:27:39.490
and meaning.

00:27:39.490 --> 00:27:41.840
Let's return finally to
the challenges facing

00:27:41.840 --> 00:27:44.669
the project of an AI that
could construct and understand

00:27:44.669 --> 00:27:46.759
meaning.

00:27:46.759 --> 00:27:48.330
What should be apparent
from my quick overview

00:27:48.330 --> 00:27:51.219
of the foundations of
animal meanings first

00:27:51.219 --> 00:27:55.379
is the wild over-optimism
of Rodney Brooks's assertion

00:27:55.379 --> 00:27:58.749
around 1990 that his robots
were approximating even

00:27:58.749 --> 00:28:01.080
insect levels of intelligence.

00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:03.590
The complexities of
bee behavior depend

00:28:03.590 --> 00:28:06.460
on modest attention,
memory, and learning, all

00:28:06.460 --> 00:28:09.450
rendered processual and
reactive to the changing world

00:28:09.450 --> 00:28:11.429
by systems of gene
regulations linked

00:28:11.429 --> 00:28:15.450
through cellular pathways
out to external stimuli.

00:28:15.450 --> 00:28:18.690
In insects, these are all causal
informational complexities.

00:28:18.690 --> 00:28:21.109
They do not manifest
the metarelation

00:28:21.109 --> 00:28:23.649
or create semiotic information.

00:28:23.649 --> 00:28:26.649
So the first step in
making a thinking machine

00:28:26.649 --> 00:28:28.840
would be to come somewhere
close to the complexities

00:28:28.840 --> 00:28:31.340
of radical niche
construction evident,

00:28:31.340 --> 00:28:34.809
even in non-semiotic creatures,
complexities that are still

00:28:34.809 --> 00:28:38.570
not fully appreciated 30 years
after Rodney Brooks's embedded,

00:28:38.570 --> 00:28:41.840
embodied, and situated AI.

00:28:41.840 --> 00:28:43.519
This would give
robots an ability

00:28:43.519 --> 00:28:45.109
to react to their
environments in ways

00:28:45.109 --> 00:28:48.349
that resemble the plastic
genomic shifts of living

00:28:48.349 --> 00:28:49.499
organisms.

00:28:49.499 --> 00:28:53.529
This is a very high bar
for machinic intelligence.

00:28:53.529 --> 00:28:55.899
Even surpassing it, however,
wouldn't endow a machine

00:28:55.899 --> 00:28:59.509
with the ability to create
aboutness and meaning.

00:28:59.509 --> 00:29:03.179
For this further leap, we'd need
to marshal systems of systems

00:29:03.179 --> 00:29:05.249
of the sort exemplified
by our songbird

00:29:05.249 --> 00:29:08.909
and then harness them to the
plastic capacity animals show

00:29:08.909 --> 00:29:11.799
to remake the systems in
moment-to-moment negotiation

00:29:11.799 --> 00:29:12.799
with their niches.

00:29:12.799 --> 00:29:16.450
The machine would need
complex attentional capacities

00:29:16.450 --> 00:29:19.360
meshing high- and
low-level processing.

00:29:19.360 --> 00:29:22.690
It would need episodic memory,
recalling whole situations

00:29:22.690 --> 00:29:25.809
in its past, and shaping its
learning on the basis of them.

00:29:25.809 --> 00:29:28.239
And all these systems would
need to submit themselves

00:29:28.239 --> 00:29:30.820
to a ceaseless alteration
on every scale,

00:29:30.820 --> 00:29:32.149
starting from the
smallest generative

00:29:32.149 --> 00:29:35.509
ones, the genes in the
machine, so to speak,

00:29:35.509 --> 00:29:37.869
which could not be
conceived as fixed entities.

00:29:37.869 --> 00:29:43.090
Now, I'm not an expert in AI or
indeed any kind of computation.

00:29:43.090 --> 00:29:45.929
My only programming experience
was a course in BASIC

00:29:45.929 --> 00:29:48.119
I took 50 years ago
at Dartmouth College,

00:29:48.119 --> 00:29:51.309
taught, as I remember it,
by John Kemeny himself,

00:29:51.309 --> 00:29:53.220
or perhaps he only
visited the class,

00:29:53.220 --> 00:29:55.419
leaving its day-to-day operation
to his graduate students.

00:29:55.419 --> 00:29:57.979
But I pose the question to
those who do know about AI,

00:29:57.979 --> 00:30:00.539
are we coming close to all this?

00:30:00.539 --> 00:30:03.759
Can all the evolutionary
algorithms, deep learning,

00:30:03.759 --> 00:30:06.610
and metaheuristics
of today's AI begin

00:30:06.610 --> 00:30:08.899
to glimpse the lifeways
of animals that bring

00:30:08.899 --> 00:30:11.019
about meaning in the world?

00:30:11.019 --> 00:30:13.279
Can they approach even
a lesser standard,

00:30:13.279 --> 00:30:16.600
the autopoietic
intelligence of a honeybee?

00:30:16.600 --> 00:30:19.279
Thank you very much.

00:30:19.279 --> 00:30:22.960
Hi, my name is Holly Herndon.

00:30:22.960 --> 00:30:26.309
I'm a composer and artist
based in Berlin working largely

00:30:26.309 --> 00:30:28.080
with music and technology.

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:31.889
I'll start this talk with a
little background about my work

00:30:31.889 --> 00:30:35.279
and then I'll talk about my
most recent project, Proto,

00:30:35.279 --> 00:30:37.609
where I'm incorporating
machine learning.

00:30:37.609 --> 00:30:42.259
So Movement was my first
album released in 2012.

00:30:42.259 --> 00:30:45.389
And for this album I built
custom digital processes

00:30:45.389 --> 00:30:47.929
for vocal performance
really in search

00:30:47.929 --> 00:30:51.599
of an embodied electronic
music performance practice.

00:30:51.599 --> 00:30:54.249
And around that time I was
coming to the conclusion

00:30:54.249 --> 00:30:57.539
that the laptop is the most
intimate instrument I have ever

00:30:57.539 --> 00:30:58.539
known.

00:30:58.539 --> 00:31:00.960
And at the time it
was still somewhat

00:31:00.960 --> 00:31:03.679
controversial to see
laptops in the concert hall

00:31:03.679 --> 00:31:09.139
as a concert instrument, so by
introducing the processed voice

00:31:09.139 --> 00:31:12.599
it created a kind of bridge
to traditional performance

00:31:12.599 --> 00:31:13.679
practice.

00:31:13.679 --> 00:31:17.940
I released Platform in
2015 as my second album

00:31:17.940 --> 00:31:21.249
and I worked with Mat Dryhurst
and Metahaven on that project.

00:31:21.249 --> 00:31:23.960
Around that time I was
coming to the conclusion

00:31:23.960 --> 00:31:27.701
that if the laptop was my
primary instrument, then

00:31:27.701 --> 00:31:31.350
the internet was really
an extended part of that.

00:31:31.350 --> 00:31:34.379
I began to focus on the
mediated relationships

00:31:34.379 --> 00:31:36.379
that I was having online.

00:31:36.379 --> 00:31:38.309
And I started making
music from sampling

00:31:38.309 --> 00:31:40.890
my own online activities.

00:31:40.890 --> 00:31:42.879
Around that time
I was also touring

00:31:42.879 --> 00:31:47.039
rather extensively off the back
of my first album, Movement.

00:31:47.039 --> 00:31:49.350
And I began to feel
like I was more at home

00:31:49.350 --> 00:31:55.450
with my email inbox than
necessarily my apartment

00:31:55.450 --> 00:31:58.169
that I would barely even see.

00:31:58.169 --> 00:32:01.830
And so in the wake of Edward
Snowden's allegations,

00:32:01.830 --> 00:32:05.200
I realized that a lot of
those really private moments

00:32:05.200 --> 00:32:09.119
and gestures might not
be so private in the end.

00:32:09.119 --> 00:32:11.960
And that every gesture made
online might hypothetically

00:32:11.960 --> 00:32:16.409
be considered a performance
for an unannounced observer.

00:32:16.409 --> 00:32:18.719
So the intimacy I had
established with Movement

00:32:18.719 --> 00:32:20.950
felt compromised or violated.

00:32:20.950 --> 00:32:24.360
But for me there was really
no turning back at that point.

00:32:24.360 --> 00:32:26.489
It already was my
primary instrument,

00:32:26.489 --> 00:32:30.799
so I really needed to understand
what this new paradigm meant

00:32:30.799 --> 00:32:32.749
for the art form.

00:32:32.749 --> 00:32:35.649
The album Proto was
released in 2019

00:32:35.649 --> 00:32:38.109
and I'm going to spend a little
bit of time talking about that

00:32:38.109 --> 00:32:41.460
and the work that led to
that I've been focusing

00:32:41.460 --> 00:32:44.220
on the last couple years.

00:32:44.220 --> 00:32:46.210
When I first started working
with machine learning

00:32:46.210 --> 00:32:48.019
I was looking into
the history a bit

00:32:48.019 --> 00:32:51.019
and I found several
examples of people creating

00:32:51.019 --> 00:32:53.210
automated composition machines.

00:32:53.210 --> 00:32:56.879
So that's this idea of training
a machine learning algorithm

00:32:56.879 --> 00:33:02.019
on Bach and then receiving
infinite variations of Bach.

00:33:02.019 --> 00:33:05.759
But I also came across
a different perspective

00:33:05.759 --> 00:33:07.749
on machine learning,
a different approach

00:33:07.749 --> 00:33:10.989
when I learned about George
Lewis and his Voyager

00:33:10.989 --> 00:33:13.860
project, which was developed
in the late '80s and early '90s

00:33:13.860 --> 00:33:14.960
at IRCAM.

00:33:14.960 --> 00:33:16.960
And so Lewis was
attempting to create

00:33:16.960 --> 00:33:21.989
a kind of ensemble member that
he could improvise alongside.

00:33:21.989 --> 00:33:24.289
So rather than
seeing the machine

00:33:24.289 --> 00:33:28.999
learning tool as a replacement
to himself as a composer,

00:33:28.999 --> 00:33:32.790
he rather saw this as
an augmentation or a way

00:33:32.790 --> 00:33:35.799
that he could perform
alongside a machine learning

00:33:35.799 --> 00:33:39.739
algorithm, which I found really
interesting and inspiring.

00:33:39.739 --> 00:33:44.259
So he was trying to embed
the musical decisions

00:33:44.259 --> 00:33:46.830
and aesthetics of the community
that he was surrounded

00:33:46.830 --> 00:33:50.779
with through the AACM and
a lot of the improv scene

00:33:50.779 --> 00:33:54.429
in African-American
circles in Chicago.

00:33:54.429 --> 00:33:59.049
And he was trying to embed
a lot of the aesthetics

00:33:59.049 --> 00:34:03.789
from this specific community
into the Voyager system.

00:34:03.789 --> 00:34:05.460
And I really love
that his work really

00:34:05.460 --> 00:34:09.129
acknowledges that aesthetics
and musical decisions

00:34:09.129 --> 00:34:10.690
actually come from somewhere.

00:34:10.690 --> 00:34:12.830
They're human cultural
decisions that

00:34:12.830 --> 00:34:16.770
come from humans interacting
with one another musically

00:34:16.770 --> 00:34:19.419
in space together.

00:34:19.419 --> 00:34:20.860
So this is our machine
learning project

00:34:20.860 --> 00:34:24.429
that we named Spawn and
this is her cover debut

00:34:24.429 --> 00:34:28.600
and her birthday
is listed as 2017.

00:34:28.600 --> 00:34:30.919
And the reason why
we use this metaphor

00:34:30.919 --> 00:34:35.780
of a baby, of an inhuman
baby, of an early life,

00:34:35.780 --> 00:34:39.700
we found this metaphor really
useful in that Spawn really

00:34:39.700 --> 00:34:41.990
only has access
to the information

00:34:41.990 --> 00:34:45.250
that we as a community
give her and really

00:34:45.250 --> 00:34:47.540
foster her with the data
that we're feeding her.

00:34:47.540 --> 00:34:50.669
So we found this as a
way to not only talk

00:34:50.669 --> 00:34:55.300
about the importance
of her daily diet,

00:34:55.300 --> 00:34:58.370
but also the communal
nature in raising

00:34:58.370 --> 00:35:01.470
this nascent intelligence.

00:35:01.470 --> 00:35:03.790
Early on, we decided to work
with audio rather than MIDI

00:35:03.790 --> 00:35:04.790
files--

00:35:04.790 --> 00:35:08.120
MIDI is a Machine
Legible Musical Score,

00:35:08.120 --> 00:35:10.470
for a few primary reasons.

00:35:10.470 --> 00:35:14.760
For one thing MIDI score loses
a lot of the performance detail

00:35:14.760 --> 00:35:16.040
that audio captures.

00:35:16.040 --> 00:35:20.530
So certain timbral and
performance expressions

00:35:20.530 --> 00:35:23.580
are really difficult to
capture in a MIDI file.

00:35:23.580 --> 00:35:27.790
Also, we didn't want to use
actual scores from the past,

00:35:27.790 --> 00:35:30.000
from ourselves or from
others, as it would encourage

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:32.960
us to make similar
compositional decisions, rather

00:35:32.960 --> 00:35:36.930
than new decisions responding
to the world around us.

00:35:36.930 --> 00:35:40.410
We view composition as a
living, breathing art form.

00:35:40.410 --> 00:35:43.580
And we feared that if
we trained on past work,

00:35:43.580 --> 00:35:47.270
we might find ourselves
in an aesthetic cul-de-sac

00:35:47.270 --> 00:35:52.200
where we're just recreating
musical decisions of the past.

00:35:52.200 --> 00:35:55.460
And lastly, we also
wanted to use our own data

00:35:55.460 --> 00:35:59.820
that we could compensate
people and give credit for.

00:35:59.820 --> 00:36:01.920
So we created all of
our own data sets.

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:03.840
There's a lot of
entitlement happening

00:36:03.840 --> 00:36:05.870
in the field of
machine learning when

00:36:05.870 --> 00:36:10.400
it comes to using other people's
data, our shared human archive.

00:36:10.400 --> 00:36:12.050
And so I'll speak about
that a little bit later,

00:36:12.050 --> 00:36:13.200
but it was really
important to us

00:36:13.200 --> 00:36:16.200
that we were able to
create our own data sets.

00:36:16.200 --> 00:36:19.130
So this is an early example
that was trained on my speaking

00:36:19.130 --> 00:36:24.410
voice and I'm using a recurrent
neural network for this.

00:36:24.410 --> 00:36:29.000
[DIGITALLY ALTERED SPEECH]

00:36:29.000 --> 00:36:38.730
I love how rough and imperfect
that early experiment was.

00:36:38.730 --> 00:36:42.600
So this led to Godmother where
it sounds like Spawn's almost

00:36:42.600 --> 00:36:45.220
beatboxing this music.

00:36:45.220 --> 00:36:47.090
Which is really funny,
because I didn't actually

00:36:47.090 --> 00:36:49.600
train the model to beatbox.

00:36:49.600 --> 00:36:52.840
But there was spoken word and
sung passages and, of course,

00:36:52.840 --> 00:36:55.890
when those two combine that
creates a kind of beatboxing.

00:36:55.890 --> 00:37:00.440
And already you can kind of hear
that Spawn can outperform me

00:37:00.440 --> 00:37:01.440
in many ways.

00:37:01.440 --> 00:37:05.120
There's no limit on the
pitch range of Spawn.

00:37:05.120 --> 00:37:08.730
I really see this as building
on some of my early experiments

00:37:08.730 --> 00:37:11.580
with digital vocal
processing, which was really

00:37:11.580 --> 00:37:14.820
a way for me to transcend some
of the physical limitations

00:37:14.820 --> 00:37:15.820
of my body.

00:37:15.820 --> 00:37:18.640
And this, for me, was
the next step in that.

00:37:18.640 --> 00:37:22.060
So to be able to create a
voice model of my own voice

00:37:22.060 --> 00:37:25.970
which didn't really have to
adhere to the laws of physics

00:37:25.970 --> 00:37:36.540
of my own body.

00:37:36.540 --> 00:37:56.260
[DIGITAL VOCALIZING]

00:37:56.260 --> 00:37:58.470
So parallel to my
work developing Spawn,

00:37:58.470 --> 00:38:01.610
I was also working with a
vocal ensemble in Berlin.

00:38:01.610 --> 00:38:04.530
Much in the lineage
of George Lewis,

00:38:04.530 --> 00:38:07.740
we decided to work with
Spawn as an ensemble member.

00:38:07.740 --> 00:38:09.830
Training her on various
members' voices,

00:38:09.830 --> 00:38:12.390
so she could perform certain
parts that we composed

00:38:12.390 --> 00:38:13.740
alongside the ensemble.

00:38:13.740 --> 00:38:16.920
So you can see her sitting on
top of the piano for this group

00:38:16.920 --> 00:38:17.920
shot.

00:38:17.920 --> 00:38:19.040
And this is about
half of the ensemble.

00:38:19.040 --> 00:38:22.060
There's around 10
members in total.

00:38:22.060 --> 00:38:23.880
As these worlds came
together, I started

00:38:23.880 --> 00:38:27.350
thinking about machine learning
as a human coordination

00:38:27.350 --> 00:38:28.350
technology.

00:38:28.350 --> 00:38:31.320
Seeing parallels to early
folk vocal techniques

00:38:31.320 --> 00:38:35.110
developed for group coordination
or cultural cohesion

00:38:35.110 --> 00:38:36.250
and ritual.

00:38:36.250 --> 00:38:38.690
And all of a sudden, a
singing AI ensemble member

00:38:38.690 --> 00:38:41.390
made perfect sense.

00:38:41.390 --> 00:38:44.810
Our ultimate goal is
to be able to perform

00:38:44.810 --> 00:38:48.410
with our machine
learning system live,

00:38:48.410 --> 00:38:50.620
but we're not quite there yet.

00:38:50.620 --> 00:38:52.110
Everything that we
recorded for the album

00:38:52.110 --> 00:38:54.040
required rendering
time that just

00:38:54.040 --> 00:38:58.610
wasn't capable of a real
time, low latency performance.

00:38:58.610 --> 00:39:02.460
So we found a way to
include a machine learning

00:39:02.460 --> 00:39:06.150
aspect to our live performances
without having that real time

00:39:06.150 --> 00:39:07.150
system.

00:39:07.150 --> 00:39:08.600
We decided to
invite the audience

00:39:08.600 --> 00:39:13.370
to train Spawn in a kind of call
and response with our ensemble.

00:39:13.370 --> 00:39:15.740
And we're really
trying to communicate

00:39:15.740 --> 00:39:19.560
that as soon as a gesture
is captured in media, photo,

00:39:19.560 --> 00:39:23.280
video, audio, whatever, it
can become machine legible

00:39:23.280 --> 00:39:25.390
and can then be part
of a training canon.

00:39:25.390 --> 00:39:28.590
We have recordings from our
audiences all over the world,

00:39:28.590 --> 00:39:32.200
so we're able to create unique
crowd models for each audience

00:39:32.200 --> 00:39:33.450
that we visit.

00:39:33.450 --> 00:39:35.170
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:39:35.170 --> 00:39:37.750
(SINGING) Our fears.

00:39:37.750 --> 00:39:39.480
Our fears.

00:39:39.480 --> 00:39:43.080
The faith will .

00:39:43.080 --> 00:39:48.440
The faith will .

00:39:48.440 --> 00:39:55.580
That's why we sing.

00:39:55.580 --> 00:40:02.730
That's why we sing.

00:40:02.730 --> 00:40:06.300
Emergent life.

00:40:06.300 --> 00:40:09.880
Emergent life.

00:40:09.880 --> 00:40:11.660
Appears.

00:40:11.660 --> 00:40:13.450
Appears.

00:40:13.450 --> 00:40:20.600
Beautiful, thank you guys.

00:40:20.600 --> 00:40:22.380
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:22.380 --> 00:40:24.820
So this is really a
huge topic to condense

00:40:24.820 --> 00:40:27.610
into such a short
talk, but I want

00:40:27.610 --> 00:40:30.410
to briefly touch on some of
the questions and ideas that

00:40:30.410 --> 00:40:33.060
arose around machine
learning and music.

00:40:33.060 --> 00:40:37.160
And I find it useful to look
at sampling as a precursor.

00:40:37.160 --> 00:40:39.040
This is an image of
Pierre Schaeffer,

00:40:39.040 --> 00:40:43.860
who's often credited as an early
pioneer of musique concrète,

00:40:43.860 --> 00:40:47.030
which was an early
recording and sampling.

00:40:47.030 --> 00:40:48.820
And Schaeffer had a
unique for the time

00:40:48.820 --> 00:40:53.470
opinion on how people might
listen to sounds decoupled

00:40:53.470 --> 00:40:54.870
from their sound source.

00:40:54.870 --> 00:40:59.000
So, for example, if you listen
to the sound of a train,

00:40:59.000 --> 00:41:01.410
the elevated listener,
in Schaeffer's mind,

00:41:01.410 --> 00:41:04.100
might be able to just think
about the aesthetic properties

00:41:04.100 --> 00:41:06.670
and the sonic qualities
of that train sound

00:41:06.670 --> 00:41:10.220
without actually imagining
the train itself.

00:41:10.220 --> 00:41:12.550
And while this is a really
interesting concept,

00:41:12.550 --> 00:41:18.170
when we apply that same idea
to human-made musical gestures,

00:41:18.170 --> 00:41:22.400
such as instrumental music or
vocal music, human created,

00:41:22.400 --> 00:41:24.420
when we decontextualized
the sound

00:41:24.420 --> 00:41:28.110
from its source we also
erase the people who

00:41:28.110 --> 00:41:30.420
made those original
sounds, we take away

00:41:30.420 --> 00:41:34.360
the accreditation component.

00:41:34.360 --> 00:41:39.271
And while sampling led to
incredible new genres of music,

00:41:39.271 --> 00:41:43.210
from hip hop to
electronica, history

00:41:43.210 --> 00:41:45.170
is often told by the victors.

00:41:45.170 --> 00:41:47.510
And there were also some
pretty egregious examples

00:41:47.510 --> 00:41:52.150
where people's work was
used and widely distributed

00:41:52.150 --> 00:41:55.340
and it was never compensated.

00:41:55.340 --> 00:41:57.490
So one of these failures
of copyright law

00:41:57.490 --> 00:42:03.370
is GC Coleman's drum break from
the 1969 song "Amen, Brother."

00:42:03.370 --> 00:42:06.350
It's probably the most
sampled drum beat in history.

00:42:06.350 --> 00:42:08.450
I'm going to play
this in a minute

00:42:08.450 --> 00:42:12.810
and if you're familiar with
genres such as breakbeat

00:42:12.810 --> 00:42:17.861
or drum and bass, this is the
essential rhythmic foundation

00:42:17.861 --> 00:42:19.700
of these entire
genres that went on

00:42:19.700 --> 00:42:22.630
to sell hundreds of
thousands of records

00:42:22.630 --> 00:42:26.170
and inspire musicians
across the world.

00:42:26.170 --> 00:42:29.590
And unfortunately, Coleman,
when he passed away he

00:42:29.590 --> 00:42:33.730
was homeless and was unaware
of the success of his sample

00:42:33.730 --> 00:42:37.060
and was never compensated
for that work.

00:42:37.060 --> 00:42:43.260
[MUSIC - GC COLEMAN, "AMEN BROTHER"]

00:42:43.260 --> 00:42:45.740
[DRUM SOLO]

00:42:45.740 --> 00:42:48.910
So the question is, how do we
not repeat those same mistakes

00:42:48.910 --> 00:42:50.990
of the past?

00:42:50.990 --> 00:42:54.500
This led me to think about this
concept of vocal sovereignty.

00:42:54.500 --> 00:42:58.440
So what does it mean
to own one's own voice?

00:42:58.440 --> 00:43:00.210
And there are a few
court cases that are

00:43:00.210 --> 00:43:01.780
interesting to reference here.

00:43:01.780 --> 00:43:04.630
For one, Bette Midler was
approached by Mercury Sable

00:43:04.630 --> 00:43:08.310
to license one of her
songs for a commercial

00:43:08.310 --> 00:43:12.690
and she rejected them, so they
hired a Bette Midler sing-alike

00:43:12.690 --> 00:43:15.100
and she was actually able
to successfully sue them

00:43:15.100 --> 00:43:17.890
that they had infringed
on her vocal likeness.

00:43:17.890 --> 00:43:19.240
And I'll just play
a quick example,

00:43:19.240 --> 00:43:23.640
so you can hear how her
vocal articulation was really

00:43:23.640 --> 00:43:26.190
mimicked by the
vocal sing-alike.

00:43:26.190 --> 00:43:29.050
MUSIC - "DO YOU WANT TO DANCE"]

00:43:29.050 --> 00:43:33.400
(SINGING) Do you want to
dance and hold my hand?

00:43:33.400 --> 00:43:36.380
Tell me you're my lover man.

00:43:36.380 --> 00:43:40.840
Do you want to dance
and hold my hand?

00:43:40.840 --> 00:43:48.100
Now, there's a car that
just asks to be driven.

00:43:48.100 --> 00:43:52.570
Tell me you're my lover.

00:43:52.570 --> 00:43:56.430
So thinking about these voice
models and vocal likeness,

00:43:56.430 --> 00:43:59.490
vocal sovereignty, made
me ask a few questions.

00:43:59.490 --> 00:44:02.590
What is an authentic
voice and who owns it?

00:44:02.590 --> 00:44:05.940
What role does influence,
inspiration, and appropriation

00:44:05.940 --> 00:44:06.940
have?

00:44:06.940 --> 00:44:08.480
How might we deal
with influence when

00:44:08.480 --> 00:44:11.180
it's automated and
can no longer operate

00:44:11.180 --> 00:44:13.930
under the alibi of inspiration?

00:44:13.930 --> 00:44:17.050
Or can a machine, in
fact, be inspired?

00:44:17.050 --> 00:44:19.660
How do styles evolve over
time and what's the best way

00:44:19.660 --> 00:44:22.150
to acknowledge influence?

00:44:22.150 --> 00:44:24.390
This slide isn't intended
to flatten these influence

00:44:24.390 --> 00:44:26.590
relationships,
but rather to show

00:44:26.590 --> 00:44:28.570
that influence comes
in different forms

00:44:28.570 --> 00:44:30.620
across generations.

00:44:30.620 --> 00:44:33.520
Not all influence
relationships are the same.

00:44:33.520 --> 00:44:35.130
What does it mean
if these influences

00:44:35.130 --> 00:44:37.180
can become fully automated?

00:44:37.180 --> 00:44:41.040
Nina Sun Eidsheim writes
in The Race of Sound:

00:44:41.040 --> 00:44:44.300
Listening, Timbre, and Vocality
in African-American Music,

00:44:44.300 --> 00:44:47.510
"voice is not singular,
it's collective.

00:44:47.510 --> 00:44:49.800
Voice is not innate,
it's cultural."

00:44:49.800 --> 00:44:52.820
She goes on to clarify,
singing is always

00:44:52.820 --> 00:44:55.330
made up of entrainment,
style, and technique,

00:44:55.330 --> 00:44:57.990
but is generally
mistaken for essence.

00:44:57.990 --> 00:45:01.310
So the voice is communal and
we perform as individuals

00:45:01.310 --> 00:45:03.640
through it.

00:45:03.640 --> 00:45:05.370
So with the history
of sampling in mind,

00:45:05.370 --> 00:45:08.050
we came up with this
idea of "spawning."

00:45:08.050 --> 00:45:12.690
How might machine learning
tools address some of the issues

00:45:12.690 --> 00:45:15.280
that sampling caused
in the last century?

00:45:15.280 --> 00:45:17.370
How might we build
in better systems

00:45:17.370 --> 00:45:19.790
from the beginning
to help avoid some

00:45:19.790 --> 00:45:22.700
of the pitfalls of the past?

00:45:22.700 --> 00:45:26.020
The idea that we came up with
we like to call "spawning."

00:45:26.020 --> 00:45:29.330
So individuals sample others
with no hard coded attribution,

00:45:29.330 --> 00:45:30.620
that's sampling.

00:45:30.620 --> 00:45:33.160
Spawning attributes
and builds upon source,

00:45:33.160 --> 00:45:35.290
or at least it could.

00:45:35.290 --> 00:45:38.580
Individuals sample others
with no hard coded payment.

00:45:38.580 --> 00:45:41.040
Spawning pays at source.

00:45:41.040 --> 00:45:43.480
Sampling repeats
expressions from the past.

00:45:43.480 --> 00:45:46.760
Spawning build's variations
from past expressions.

00:45:46.760 --> 00:45:49.160
Sampling is one-to-one
reproduction.

00:45:49.160 --> 00:45:51.610
Spawning a smart
and reproductive.

00:45:51.610 --> 00:45:54.860
Given the enormous capabilities
and possibilities of machine

00:45:54.860 --> 00:45:56.800
learning models and
some of the questions

00:45:56.800 --> 00:45:59.120
I brought up around
vocal sovereignty,

00:45:59.120 --> 00:46:01.230
we think it's important to be
thinking about these issues

00:46:01.230 --> 00:46:03.100
from the start.

00:46:03.100 --> 00:46:05.090
One idea I've been developing
is called "Hitmaker,"

00:46:05.090 --> 00:46:08.400
and it's a project in
collaboration with JJ

00:46:08.400 --> 00:46:09.950
Burred and Mathew Dryhurst.

00:46:09.950 --> 00:46:12.560
And so this is a
contemporary take

00:46:12.560 --> 00:46:15.640
on a sampler using
machine learning.

00:46:15.640 --> 00:46:17.970
So the idea is is
you have a source,

00:46:17.970 --> 00:46:20.360
and then as soon as you load
that source into the tool

00:46:20.360 --> 00:46:24.050
it's registered, so it
can be automatically given

00:46:24.050 --> 00:46:25.130
attribution.

00:46:25.130 --> 00:46:27.260
And then the next step
leads to a sound separation

00:46:27.260 --> 00:46:34.220
and spawning, which I'll
demonstrate briefly here.

00:46:34.220 --> 00:46:43.620
[MUSIC - "HITMAKER"]

00:46:43.620 --> 00:46:48.320
[VOCALIZING]

00:46:48.320 --> 00:46:50.850
So first we separate the
kick and the snare sound

00:46:50.850 --> 00:46:53.070
using a sound
separation algorithm.

00:46:53.070 --> 00:46:54.650
[DRUM BEATS]

00:46:54.650 --> 00:46:57.500
And using those
as a target sound,

00:46:57.500 --> 00:47:02.030
we're able to spawn or
create new variations

00:47:02.030 --> 00:47:17.100
on those original sounds.

00:47:17.100 --> 00:47:43.430
[DRUM BEATS]

00:47:43.430 --> 00:47:45.930
So we're obviously working with
very simple percussive sounds

00:47:45.930 --> 00:47:47.530
in this instance,
but you could imagine

00:47:47.530 --> 00:47:49.640
how one might extrapolate
other instrument

00:47:49.640 --> 00:47:53.140
sounds from an original source.

00:47:53.140 --> 00:47:54.250
This is another
project that we're

00:47:54.250 --> 00:47:57.870
working on using Google
Magenta's DDSP system, which

00:47:57.870 --> 00:48:00.930
can transfer timbre from
one instrument to another.

00:48:00.930 --> 00:48:03.790
We're currently trying to make
the system work for real time

00:48:03.790 --> 00:48:04.790
performance.

00:48:04.790 --> 00:48:08.210
This is an example of a voice
being transformed into a flute.

00:48:08.210 --> 00:48:10.840
This is a collaboration I worked
on with Maria Arnal, who's

00:48:10.840 --> 00:48:14.220
a Catalan vocalist, and this is
the "Song of Sybil" a cappella.

00:48:14.220 --> 00:48:16.320
And then I'll play
the flute version.

00:48:16.320 --> 00:48:19.390
So pay close attention to
the ornaments in her vocal

00:48:19.390 --> 00:48:27.160
and see how closely they
align with the flute version.

00:48:27.160 --> 00:49:01.120
[MUSIC - MARIA ARNAL, "SONG OF SYBIL"]
[VOCALIZING]

00:49:01.120 --> 00:49:10.830
[FLUTE INSTRUMENTAL]

00:49:10.830 --> 00:49:13.620
And lastly, I'm working on
an opensource model version

00:49:13.620 --> 00:49:16.010
of my voice that I'll be
able to share with the wider

00:49:16.010 --> 00:49:18.130
public in about a month.

00:49:18.130 --> 00:49:20.890
Unfortunately, I can't
share that yet at this time,

00:49:20.890 --> 00:49:23.480
but I'm really excited
to-- for that to come out,

00:49:23.480 --> 00:49:25.570
so please stay tuned for that.

00:49:25.570 --> 00:49:27.660
For those who are interested,
some of the questions

00:49:27.660 --> 00:49:29.640
that came up through the
making of this last album

00:49:29.640 --> 00:49:33.310
and some of the new tools that
we're developing at the moment,

00:49:33.310 --> 00:49:35.370
I discuss these issues
on a weekly basis

00:49:35.370 --> 00:49:39.070
with my partner, Mat Dryhurst,
on our podcast Interdependence.

00:49:39.070 --> 00:49:41.240
The idea is that we're
speaking with people

00:49:41.240 --> 00:49:43.870
who we think are shaping
21st century culture, often

00:49:43.870 --> 00:49:46.500
with a focus on the
impact of technology.

00:49:46.500 --> 00:49:49.080
Thank you so much for your time.

00:49:49.080 --> 00:49:52.790
Our visual system is amazing.

00:49:52.790 --> 00:49:55.040
We can make sense of
the world around us

00:49:55.040 --> 00:49:58.710
just by analyzing the light that
bounces off objects and reaches

00:49:58.710 --> 00:50:01.260
our eyes.

00:50:01.260 --> 00:50:05.270
To illustrate this, let
me show you a movie.

00:50:05.270 --> 00:50:10.000
This will help you to
appreciate how amazing we are.

00:50:10.000 --> 00:50:13.580
This following video is
showing a very low quality

00:50:13.580 --> 00:50:15.460
movie recorded in my office.

00:50:15.460 --> 00:50:18.300
This movie is so blurry
that many of the objects

00:50:18.300 --> 00:50:21.390
are defined by only
one single pixel.

00:50:21.390 --> 00:50:23.080
But our visual
system is so amazing

00:50:23.080 --> 00:50:25.520
that we can recognize a
person talking on the phone,

00:50:25.520 --> 00:50:29.850
listening to some music,
and working on a computer.

00:50:29.850 --> 00:50:32.150
And it is amazing that
we are able to recognize

00:50:32.150 --> 00:50:36.150
so much from so little
visual information.

00:50:36.150 --> 00:50:39.370
Of course, most of what
we saw in this video

00:50:39.370 --> 00:50:41.300
was not really there.

00:50:41.300 --> 00:50:44.850
Most of what we saw in the
video was only inside our heads.

00:50:44.850 --> 00:50:47.460
And to illustrate
this, let's watch

00:50:47.460 --> 00:50:52.660
the original high
resolution movie.

00:50:52.660 --> 00:50:55.390
As you can see now,
many of the objects

00:50:55.390 --> 00:51:07.910
that you recognized before were
not really what you thought.

00:51:07.910 --> 00:51:10.950
So why is it so difficult
to build a machine that sees

00:51:10.950 --> 00:51:14.130
the same ways that humans do?

00:51:14.130 --> 00:51:18.010
Well, the input to a
vision system, an image,

00:51:18.010 --> 00:51:21.500
is just a collection of numbers
like the one shown here.

00:51:21.500 --> 00:51:26.620
Its number represents a pixel
intensity at that location.

00:51:26.620 --> 00:51:28.640
The numbers do not
directly tell us

00:51:28.640 --> 00:51:30.290
which objects are
in the picture,

00:51:30.290 --> 00:51:32.880
or even which numbers
are part of an object

00:51:32.880 --> 00:51:37.110
or part of the background.

00:51:37.110 --> 00:51:39.000
This is what a machine will see.

00:51:39.000 --> 00:51:42.680
And what we see here, if
we look at the same numbers

00:51:42.680 --> 00:51:44.890
but displayed in the
form of a picture,

00:51:44.890 --> 00:51:49.820
that we see just a blurry
picture of a person walking.

00:51:49.820 --> 00:51:52.370
Computer vision systems need
to be designed and trained

00:51:52.370 --> 00:51:55.290
to see the person
behind the numbers.

00:51:55.290 --> 00:51:58.660
And that's where the
challenge resides.

00:51:58.660 --> 00:52:01.320
Training a system capable of
recognizing objects and images

00:52:01.320 --> 00:52:04.870
generally requires
annotating manually

00:52:04.870 --> 00:52:07.300
thousands of images
where we will

00:52:07.300 --> 00:52:09.620
have to indicate
what are the objects

00:52:09.620 --> 00:52:12.630
in each picture,
their boundaries,

00:52:12.630 --> 00:52:14.080
and what their name is.

00:52:14.080 --> 00:52:17.820
And the system will then
learn to associate the image

00:52:17.820 --> 00:52:19.810
with the annotations
that we provided.

00:52:19.810 --> 00:52:22.650
And this process is
very labor intensive,

00:52:22.650 --> 00:52:28.940
and it requires many hundreds
of hours of manual labor.

00:52:28.940 --> 00:52:32.960
A different strategy that is
currently used in research

00:52:32.960 --> 00:52:36.960
is to train systems
using computer graphics.

00:52:36.960 --> 00:52:41.300
So for instance, the community
is developing virtual worlds

00:52:41.300 --> 00:52:45.640
where you can interact with
agents, you can see the world,

00:52:45.640 --> 00:52:49.060
recognize objects,
and train AI systems

00:52:49.060 --> 00:52:51.650
in these environments
with the hope

00:52:51.650 --> 00:52:55.250
that later they will learn
something that can be

00:52:55.250 --> 00:52:58.000
transferred to the real world.

00:52:58.000 --> 00:53:01.310
But all these different
ways of training

00:53:01.310 --> 00:53:03.380
artificial visual
systems are very

00:53:03.380 --> 00:53:08.000
different to how babies actually
learn to perceive the world.

00:53:08.000 --> 00:53:10.571
And so let's watch a
baby actually learning

00:53:10.571 --> 00:53:16.650
to recognize things
in the world.

00:53:16.650 --> 00:53:19.110
A baby learns most of
the time by itself.

00:53:19.110 --> 00:53:21.720
There is no supervision
from the parents.

00:53:21.720 --> 00:53:23.690
There are no labels.

00:53:23.690 --> 00:53:26.910
And he learns by
interacting with the world,

00:53:26.910 --> 00:53:30.010
by looking at things, by hearing
the noise that they make,

00:53:30.010 --> 00:53:35.010
by touching them, and
finally, tasting them.

00:53:35.010 --> 00:53:37.850
And a baby will learn
to discover objects,

00:53:37.850 --> 00:53:40.630
to understand materials,
to perceive a space,

00:53:40.630 --> 00:53:45.260
to manipulate objects
via interaction.

00:53:45.260 --> 00:53:48.070
And it will do this
through a very rich set

00:53:48.070 --> 00:53:51.740
of sensory modalities--
vision, audition, smell, taste,

00:53:51.740 --> 00:53:52.760
and touch.

00:53:52.760 --> 00:53:57.750
And he does this in a
completely unsupervised way.

00:53:57.750 --> 00:54:00.800
It doesn't require the type
of exhaustive annotations

00:54:00.800 --> 00:54:06.410
that current AI systems need
to learn about the world.

00:54:06.410 --> 00:54:08.410
So in this presentation,
I will describe

00:54:08.410 --> 00:54:12.530
some of the work in our
groups, trying to build systems

00:54:12.530 --> 00:54:15.630
that leverage different sensory
modalities to learn to perceive

00:54:15.630 --> 00:54:19.600
the world without supervision.

00:54:19.600 --> 00:54:24.640
So we started our work in 2016
with a very simple set in.

00:54:24.640 --> 00:54:26.870
Andrew Owens, a
PhD student at MIT,

00:54:26.870 --> 00:54:32.750
recorded video and sounds
while he was hitting surfaces

00:54:32.750 --> 00:54:34.430
with a drumstick.

00:54:34.430 --> 00:54:36.510
For about a month,
he walked around

00:54:36.510 --> 00:54:39.760
with the camera and a
drumstick and recorded

00:54:39.760 --> 00:54:42.020
many thousands of hits.

00:54:42.020 --> 00:55:03.970
So let me show some
of his greatest hits.

00:55:03.970 --> 00:55:07.150
He hit and scratched
surfaces all over MIT

00:55:07.150 --> 00:55:09.250
for a long, long time.

00:55:09.250 --> 00:55:15.740
And he recorded tens of
thousands of these events.

00:55:15.740 --> 00:55:19.740
So with this data set, we
asked the following question.

00:55:19.740 --> 00:55:23.240
Given the video input
without the audio--

00:55:23.240 --> 00:55:27.070
just the pixels-- is
it possible to predict

00:55:27.070 --> 00:55:29.690
what the audio should be?

00:55:29.690 --> 00:55:32.640
So we want to build a system
that will take this input--

00:55:32.640 --> 00:55:35.200
just the pixels, just
the frames of the video--

00:55:35.200 --> 00:55:41.940
and it should produce the
sounds that should go with it.

00:55:41.940 --> 00:55:44.869
A system trained to solve
this audio prediction task

00:55:44.869 --> 00:55:48.070
should be able to solve
the following problems.

00:55:48.070 --> 00:55:50.630
It will have to
recognize the drumstick.

00:55:50.630 --> 00:55:52.130
And it will have
to detect when it

00:55:52.130 --> 00:55:54.100
is in contact with the
surface and whether it

00:55:54.100 --> 00:55:56.740
was a hit or a scratch.

00:55:56.740 --> 00:55:58.500
It will have to recognize
the type of contact--

00:55:58.500 --> 00:56:02.260
the strength and the
duration of the contact.

00:56:02.260 --> 00:56:03.920
It will have to
recognize the material

00:56:03.920 --> 00:56:06.520
where the contact happened.

00:56:06.520 --> 00:56:09.510
And finally, it will have
to associate all of this

00:56:09.510 --> 00:56:13.820
with the sounds that you hear.

00:56:13.820 --> 00:56:14.820
So we did that task.

00:56:14.820 --> 00:56:17.820
We trained a system
to solve this problem.

00:56:17.820 --> 00:56:21.369
And what I will show
you now is the result

00:56:21.369 --> 00:56:25.650
of running that system
on a silent video.

00:56:25.650 --> 00:56:27.940
So what you will
see, the input video,

00:56:27.940 --> 00:56:31.400
shows the drumstick hitting
and scratching a rock.

00:56:31.400 --> 00:56:34.960
But the system will only see
the images, not the audio.

00:56:34.960 --> 00:56:38.060
What you will hear,
as I play this video,

00:56:38.060 --> 00:56:39.980
is the audio predicted
by the system.

00:56:39.980 --> 00:56:42.910
It's not the real audio
that came with the video.

00:56:42.910 --> 00:56:51.140
So let's play this.

00:56:51.140 --> 00:56:54.050
And as you can hear,
the sound was currently,

00:56:54.050 --> 00:56:56.510
temporarily aligned with the
moments in which the drumstick

00:56:56.510 --> 00:57:00.550
was in contact with the surface,
and the sounds corresponded

00:57:00.550 --> 00:57:01.850
to hits and scratches.

00:57:01.850 --> 00:57:04.040
It was not perfect.

00:57:04.040 --> 00:57:06.840
But the system shows that
it has learned something

00:57:06.840 --> 00:57:08.800
about the vessel
and other worlds

00:57:08.800 --> 00:57:13.060
without any human supervision

00:57:13.060 --> 00:57:15.270
So what has the system learned?

00:57:15.270 --> 00:57:18.530
The network has learned to
represent surface properties

00:57:18.530 --> 00:57:20.890
from visual information only.

00:57:20.890 --> 00:57:22.880
That allows to discriminate
among different types

00:57:22.880 --> 00:57:24.100
of materials.

00:57:24.100 --> 00:57:29.390
A simple classifier trained
on the self-learned features

00:57:29.390 --> 00:57:33.290
can discriminate among
different types of materials.

00:57:33.290 --> 00:57:36.420
It will confuse materials that
produce similar sounds when

00:57:36.420 --> 00:57:39.680
hit with a drumstick, such
as glass and metal, which

00:57:39.680 --> 00:57:44.660
sound very similar when you
hit them with a drumstick.

00:57:44.660 --> 00:57:46.850
But learning with
sounds and images

00:57:46.850 --> 00:57:49.580
can be done even without
having to hit everything

00:57:49.580 --> 00:57:51.500
with a drumstick.

00:57:51.500 --> 00:57:54.280
In fact, when we are
in a particular place,

00:57:54.280 --> 00:57:58.020
anywhere, we will
always hear some sounds.

00:57:58.020 --> 00:58:01.100
The ambient sound
that surrounds us

00:58:01.100 --> 00:58:02.870
is a mixture of
all of the sounds

00:58:02.870 --> 00:58:07.520
that are made by the objects
that are present in the scene.

00:58:07.520 --> 00:58:09.960
So it's like thinking
that the world comes

00:58:09.960 --> 00:58:13.140
with its own soundtrack.

00:58:13.140 --> 00:58:14.960
And many objects in the
world produce sounds

00:58:14.960 --> 00:58:18.160
by themselves of
different kinds.

00:58:18.160 --> 00:58:21.940
And the main cue that
we could use here

00:58:21.940 --> 00:58:25.630
is the temporal coincidence
of hearing a particular sound

00:58:25.630 --> 00:58:29.640
and seeing a particular
visual pattern in the world.

00:58:29.640 --> 00:58:31.660
And then we can
create an association

00:58:31.660 --> 00:58:35.340
between that sound that we
hear and that particular visual

00:58:35.340 --> 00:58:36.984
pattern and the
fact that they seem

00:58:36.984 --> 00:58:42.619
to co-occur time over time.

00:58:42.619 --> 00:58:45.220
So we can now play the same
crush model prediction game

00:58:45.220 --> 00:58:46.520
that we did for the drumstick.

00:58:46.520 --> 00:58:49.600
We will try to predict
what type of sounds

00:58:49.600 --> 00:58:52.520
are associated with
a particular image.

00:58:52.520 --> 00:58:55.240
And in this case, the image
will not contain the drumstick.

00:58:55.240 --> 00:58:57.280
It's just whatever
objects are there

00:58:57.280 --> 00:58:58.840
that are producing sounds.

00:58:58.840 --> 00:59:03.090
And a model trained to solve
this task, if it is successful,

00:59:03.090 --> 00:59:05.230
it will have to solve
the following problem.

00:59:05.230 --> 00:59:09.170
It will have to be able to
recognize and categorize

00:59:09.170 --> 00:59:12.450
the objects that are in the
scene that are naturally

00:59:12.450 --> 00:59:14.480
associated with ambient sounds.

00:59:14.480 --> 00:59:16.100
And it will have
to associate them

00:59:16.100 --> 00:59:18.550
with the appropriate
type of sound that

00:59:18.550 --> 00:59:21.650
goes with that object.

00:59:21.650 --> 00:59:25.250
So what has a network
like that learned?

00:59:25.250 --> 00:59:28.040
We can now, once we
train such a system,

00:59:28.040 --> 00:59:32.650
we can look inside what
the system has learned.

00:59:32.650 --> 00:59:35.500
Just in a similar way to
how a neuroscientist would

00:59:35.500 --> 00:59:37.240
look inside the brain--

00:59:37.240 --> 00:59:39.190
look at the activation
of the neurons

00:59:39.190 --> 00:59:41.520
to try to understand
how the brain works--

00:59:41.520 --> 00:59:45.100
we can perform the same thing
on a neural network that has

00:59:45.100 --> 00:59:47.060
been trying to solve this task.

00:59:47.060 --> 00:59:52.430
And what we discover is
that inside the network,

00:59:52.430 --> 00:59:55.280
some of the neurons
became specialized

00:59:55.280 --> 00:59:59.640
in detecting baby faces or
those units became specialized

00:59:59.640 --> 01:00:02.310
in detecting crowds of people.

01:00:02.310 --> 01:00:04.400
And this is because baby faces--

01:00:04.400 --> 01:00:06.310
babies produce a
particular type of sound.

01:00:06.310 --> 01:00:08.660
And whenever a baby
face was present,

01:00:08.660 --> 01:00:13.510
that was the sound that
was associated with it.

01:00:13.510 --> 01:00:17.280
Crowds also made a
different type of noise.

01:00:17.280 --> 01:00:19.330
And there are other
units that are selected

01:00:19.330 --> 01:00:22.540
to waterfalls, the ocean.

01:00:22.540 --> 01:00:27.610
And all of those
units have learned

01:00:27.610 --> 01:00:30.440
to detect objects
in images that are

01:00:30.440 --> 01:00:32.600
associated with distinctive
types of sounds.

01:00:32.600 --> 01:00:34.410
There are units that
will detect also

01:00:34.410 --> 01:00:38.940
the sky because it comes
with the sound of the wind.

01:00:38.940 --> 01:00:41.710
And also, cars.

01:00:41.710 --> 01:00:44.390
The important point
here is that these units

01:00:44.390 --> 01:00:46.800
have learned to
detect these objects,

01:00:46.800 --> 01:00:50.030
without acquiring any
type of human supervision,

01:00:50.030 --> 01:00:52.910
just by learning from
the temporal association

01:00:52.910 --> 01:00:56.010
between sounds and
visual patterns.

01:00:56.010 --> 01:00:59.910
In particular, for instance,
the car selective unit

01:00:59.910 --> 01:01:03.280
is capable of detecting
cars, but it has never

01:01:03.280 --> 01:01:10.220
been trained with a human having
to annotate cars in images.

01:01:10.220 --> 01:01:15.430
Sensing images and audio
has a long history.

01:01:15.430 --> 01:01:18.520
But there are other types of
sensitive sensory modalities.

01:01:18.520 --> 01:01:21.850
And in particular, one very
important sensory modality

01:01:21.850 --> 01:01:25.880
for humans, for which there does
not exist a mature technology,

01:01:25.880 --> 01:01:27.540
is touch.

01:01:27.540 --> 01:01:30.410
Tactile information is
essential for humans,

01:01:30.410 --> 01:01:32.540
with a significant
part of our brains

01:01:32.540 --> 01:01:35.520
the ability to process
tactile information recorded

01:01:35.520 --> 01:01:38.260
by our skin.

01:01:38.260 --> 01:01:41.840
And there are a
number of devices--

01:01:41.840 --> 01:01:43.580
commercial devices
and research devices--

01:01:43.580 --> 01:01:48.120
that have developed to
measure tactile information.

01:01:48.120 --> 01:01:50.440
But it is still a
very small number.

01:01:50.440 --> 01:01:54.070
Those devices are nowhere
near the quality of maturity

01:01:54.070 --> 01:01:57.500
that cameras and
microphones have reached.

01:01:57.500 --> 01:02:02.859
We are developing
tactile sensors

01:02:02.859 --> 01:02:08.390
that will also record tactile
information when a human

01:02:08.390 --> 01:02:09.560
interacts with an object.

01:02:09.560 --> 01:02:10.930
In this particular
case, it's a glove

01:02:10.930 --> 01:02:16.520
that a human can wear that
records pressure signals.

01:02:16.520 --> 01:02:18.750
And as you are
manipulating an object,

01:02:18.750 --> 01:02:20.890
it will record which
parts of the hand

01:02:20.890 --> 01:02:23.360
are in contact with
the object and what is

01:02:23.360 --> 01:02:25.550
the strength of that contact.

01:02:25.550 --> 01:02:28.770
This way, we can
develop approaches

01:02:28.770 --> 01:02:32.040
that can use this measured
tactile information to perform

01:02:32.040 --> 01:02:40.380
object recognition, to measure
object properties, and so on.

01:02:40.380 --> 01:02:43.360
And it's not just
about the hand.

01:02:43.360 --> 01:02:45.310
The same technology
can be extended

01:02:45.310 --> 01:02:48.040
to cover large body
surfaces, and eventually

01:02:48.040 --> 01:02:52.840
become a tactile-sensing
skin for robots.

01:02:52.840 --> 01:02:58.010
So as I mentioned, all these
different sensory modalities

01:02:58.010 --> 01:03:01.170
is what provides,
now, a way of learning

01:03:01.170 --> 01:03:03.780
from the temporal coincidences
between different events

01:03:03.780 --> 01:03:05.410
across different senses.

01:03:05.410 --> 01:03:10.030
And this will provide a
method to learn about objects

01:03:10.030 --> 01:03:21.880
without requiring
human annotations.

01:03:21.880 --> 01:03:23.430
Besides interacting
with the world,

01:03:23.430 --> 01:03:25.300
there are many
other ways in which

01:03:25.300 --> 01:03:29.720
we learn to recognize things.

01:03:29.720 --> 01:03:31.710
So let's think of
the following game.

01:03:31.710 --> 01:03:35.520
Let's say that I give
you lots of images,

01:03:35.520 --> 01:03:38.760
and you have to play
the following game.

01:03:38.760 --> 01:03:40.341
Given a collection
of images, I want

01:03:40.341 --> 01:03:45.150
you to draw a new image that
looks similar to the images

01:03:45.150 --> 01:03:46.420
that I gave you.

01:03:46.420 --> 01:03:49.560
It doesn't need to be one of
the images that I gave you,

01:03:49.560 --> 01:03:54.510
but it has to look as
belonging to the same family.

01:03:54.510 --> 01:03:57.000
Playing this game
requires understanding

01:03:57.000 --> 01:04:00.170
what the content of
these pictures is,

01:04:00.170 --> 01:04:01.850
understanding
through the geometry,

01:04:01.850 --> 01:04:06.660
and then drawing something
that looks similar to it.

01:04:06.660 --> 01:04:11.070
And current systems--
current neural networks

01:04:11.070 --> 01:04:14.250
have been tackling this
problem for some time.

01:04:14.250 --> 01:04:17.410
And there has been a lot of
progress in recent years.

01:04:17.410 --> 01:04:21.410
There is a family
of systems called

01:04:21.410 --> 01:04:25.710
generative adversarial networks
that try to solve that problem.

01:04:25.710 --> 01:04:27.420
Given a bunch of
images, they want

01:04:27.420 --> 01:04:33.760
to be able to draw new
images that look real.

01:04:33.760 --> 01:04:35.810
This approach was
proposed in 2014,

01:04:35.810 --> 01:04:38.610
and these are the
type of images that

01:04:38.610 --> 01:04:42.660
were produced by an artificial
system trying to reproduce

01:04:42.660 --> 01:04:45.800
images that seem real.

01:04:45.800 --> 01:04:49.040
And as you can see, they
were very low quality--

01:04:49.040 --> 01:04:51.310
very similar to the blurry
movie that I showed you

01:04:51.310 --> 01:04:52.510
at the beginning.

01:04:52.510 --> 01:04:56.700
Two years later,
new advances were

01:04:56.700 --> 01:05:00.160
capable of drawing
images like the ones

01:05:00.160 --> 01:05:04.260
that I'm showing here, which are
trying to look like bedrooms.

01:05:04.260 --> 01:05:06.860
They are not very
good, but you can

01:05:06.860 --> 01:05:09.580
see that there has been
a big step forward.

01:05:09.580 --> 01:05:11.790
And there are some
geometric figures

01:05:11.790 --> 01:05:15.430
that look like they could
come from the real world.

01:05:15.430 --> 01:05:17.450
Although, no one
would get will get

01:05:17.450 --> 01:05:20.360
fooled in thinking that
these are real images.

01:05:20.360 --> 01:05:23.710
But just two years
later, the advances

01:05:23.710 --> 01:05:27.210
were so big that systems
were capable of producing

01:05:27.210 --> 01:05:33.980
images that looked like this
and therefore quite real.

01:05:33.980 --> 01:05:36.820
And since then, there has
been a lot of progress.

01:05:36.820 --> 01:05:38.450
So the system, what
it does is start

01:05:38.450 --> 01:05:40.520
with a collection of
images like these ones

01:05:40.520 --> 01:05:44.090
that contains buildings,
churches, and castles.

01:05:44.090 --> 01:05:46.940
And from this
collection, it will

01:05:46.940 --> 01:05:51.650
train to be able to generate new
images that should look real.

01:05:51.650 --> 01:05:54.780
The way in which this
neural network works is it

01:05:54.780 --> 01:05:58.200
will take as input
just a random number--

01:05:58.200 --> 01:06:00.270
a random collection of
numbers, like flipping

01:06:00.270 --> 01:06:03.869
a coin 512, 500, times--

01:06:03.869 --> 01:06:06.960
and recording just the outcomes.

01:06:06.960 --> 01:06:12.700
This vector will enter
into a neural network that

01:06:12.700 --> 01:06:16.800
will recombine this set of
numbers, make it bigger,

01:06:16.800 --> 01:06:20.950
recombine again, enhance it,
recombine it, until at the end,

01:06:20.950 --> 01:06:22.650
you end up with a picture.

01:06:22.650 --> 01:06:26.670
And this picture is a
randomly generated image.

01:06:26.670 --> 01:06:32.440
If you throw the coin multiple
times, different times,

01:06:32.440 --> 01:06:37.930
you will get a
different set of images.

01:06:37.930 --> 01:06:41.000
So these are examples of images
that are not real images.

01:06:41.000 --> 01:06:43.350
They are generated by
this neural network,

01:06:43.350 --> 01:06:44.650
and they look amazing.

01:06:44.650 --> 01:06:46.660
They even have
watermarks because they

01:06:46.660 --> 01:06:48.950
were part of the trainings.

01:06:48.950 --> 01:06:54.930
But the question is, to render
images that look so real,

01:06:54.930 --> 01:06:57.750
what is it this neural
network has really learned?

01:06:57.750 --> 01:06:59.500
Does it know about objects?

01:06:59.500 --> 01:07:04.190
How it is capable of putting
the pixels in the right places,

01:07:04.190 --> 01:07:08.030
so that they look as a
sky, trees, and buildings?

01:07:08.030 --> 01:07:10.690
Is there a representation
that is explicit,

01:07:10.690 --> 01:07:16.150
and it contains some
of these objects?

01:07:16.150 --> 01:07:18.369
So that's a question
that we asked ourselves,

01:07:18.369 --> 01:07:22.050
and we started looking
inside the neural network

01:07:22.050 --> 01:07:25.890
to see if it was discovering
some of these objects.

01:07:25.890 --> 01:07:30.620
And, indeed, what we
found is that there

01:07:30.620 --> 01:07:33.761
are units inside the
network that have learned

01:07:33.761 --> 01:07:37.510
to draw a specific object.

01:07:37.510 --> 01:07:42.740
For instance, in a particular
layer-- layer number four,

01:07:42.740 --> 01:07:45.440
neuron 119--

01:07:45.440 --> 01:07:48.750
became a tree-specific unit.

01:07:48.750 --> 01:07:51.350
Whenever that unit
was activated, at tree

01:07:51.350 --> 01:07:54.960
will appear on the picture.

01:07:54.960 --> 01:07:59.010
Another unit-- unit number 43--

01:07:59.010 --> 01:08:00.790
whenever that neuron
was activated,

01:08:00.790 --> 01:08:05.630
a dome will appear on the
picture, like in these images.

01:08:05.630 --> 01:08:09.250
There was another neuron--

01:08:09.250 --> 01:08:13.220
neuron 84-- that was
specialized in drawing windows.

01:08:13.220 --> 01:08:15.480
Whenever, that
neuron was activated,

01:08:15.480 --> 01:08:19.130
a window will appear
on the picture.

01:08:19.130 --> 01:08:23.179
And unit 315 was specialized
in throwing chairs.

01:08:23.179 --> 01:08:25.809
And whenever that unit
will be activated,

01:08:25.809 --> 01:08:31.049
a chair will appear
on the picture.

01:08:31.049 --> 01:08:34.940
So what is really interesting
is that these units that

01:08:34.940 --> 01:08:38.779
are explaining some objects
appearing or disappearing

01:08:38.779 --> 01:08:40.549
on the picture--
now we can intervene

01:08:40.549 --> 01:08:43.679
on this neural network
and control what

01:08:43.679 --> 01:08:46.079
we want to have on the picture.

01:08:46.079 --> 01:08:48.900
So for instance, this image,
which is randomly generated

01:08:48.900 --> 01:08:51.519
by the neural network--

01:08:51.519 --> 01:08:54.210
if we take the
units that we know

01:08:54.210 --> 01:08:57.969
are responsible for drawing
windows and we turn them off.

01:08:57.969 --> 01:09:00.789
We tell them not to
get activated anymore

01:09:00.789 --> 01:09:05.670
in this picture, and then we
render the resulting image,

01:09:05.670 --> 01:09:09.299
what we get is something
like this picture, which is

01:09:09.299 --> 01:09:11.250
very similar to the other one.

01:09:11.250 --> 01:09:13.329
But it doesn't have windows.

01:09:13.329 --> 01:09:16.449
And instead, it has replaced
the window with a wall,

01:09:16.449 --> 01:09:20.940
and there seems to be
a painting on the wall.

01:09:20.940 --> 01:09:22.999
And the only thing
that we have done

01:09:22.999 --> 01:09:26.650
is to manually
turn off the units

01:09:26.650 --> 01:09:29.499
that we believe are responsible
for drawing windows.

01:09:29.499 --> 01:09:33.529
Here is another example
of a different image that

01:09:33.529 --> 01:09:36.579
is drawing a conference room.

01:09:36.579 --> 01:09:41.790
If we turn off the window units,
the windows in the background

01:09:41.790 --> 01:09:42.790
disappear.

01:09:42.790 --> 01:09:44.940
And they get
replaced by diplomas,

01:09:44.940 --> 01:09:49.009
which seems to be an appropriate
decoration for a conference

01:09:49.009 --> 01:09:50.369
room.

01:09:50.369 --> 01:09:53.150
All these images are
completely randomly generated

01:09:53.150 --> 01:09:56.030
by this neural network.

01:09:56.030 --> 01:09:57.829
We can also ask
the neural network

01:09:57.829 --> 01:10:00.280
to do things that
are impossible.

01:10:00.280 --> 01:10:03.630
For instance, this
picture is very great,

01:10:03.630 --> 01:10:04.809
and we want to have more color.

01:10:04.809 --> 01:10:07.469
We can turn on
neurons that we have

01:10:07.469 --> 01:10:11.260
found are responsible
for drawing grass.

01:10:11.260 --> 01:10:13.739
But we are going to activate
those neurons everywhere

01:10:13.739 --> 01:10:14.979
on the picture.

01:10:14.979 --> 01:10:18.440
And then the result is
something like this,

01:10:18.440 --> 01:10:24.039
where it seems as if humanity
is gone after a thousand years.

01:10:24.039 --> 01:10:28.369
And you can see that grass
is growing everywhere.

01:10:28.369 --> 01:10:30.579
It's growing on top
of the building.

01:10:30.579 --> 01:10:34.070
And these are things that
this neural network never

01:10:34.070 --> 01:10:35.800
saw during training.

01:10:35.800 --> 01:10:38.460
It's putting objects
in places where

01:10:38.460 --> 01:10:41.989
we are asking it to do
it, but this is something

01:10:41.989 --> 01:10:45.139
that the network never
saw during training.

01:10:45.139 --> 01:10:48.380
So the network seems to have
learned to represent objects

01:10:48.380 --> 01:10:51.809
explicitly, despite
that, again, that there

01:10:51.809 --> 01:10:55.179
were no human annotations.

01:10:55.179 --> 01:10:59.449
No human had to indicate what
the window is, what a car is,

01:10:59.449 --> 01:11:00.519
what the grass is.

01:11:00.519 --> 01:11:05.590
The network has learned to
associate parts of the network

01:11:05.590 --> 01:11:09.909
with specific objects.

01:11:09.909 --> 01:11:12.960
So this is part
of this enterprise

01:11:12.960 --> 01:11:16.409
of trying to learn
without supervision,

01:11:16.409 --> 01:11:18.369
discovering what are
the objects in the world

01:11:18.369 --> 01:11:23.179
through different
modalities and imagination,

01:11:23.179 --> 01:11:28.469
so that you do not require a
teacher or human annotators

01:11:28.469 --> 01:11:34.310
in order to train an
artificial system.

01:11:34.310 --> 01:11:35.460
My name is Markus Buehler.

01:11:35.460 --> 01:11:38.590
I am the McAfee Professor
of Engineering at MIT,

01:11:38.590 --> 01:11:41.309
and today I'll be presenting
some of our recent research

01:11:41.309 --> 01:11:43.870
on the Molecular Mechanics
of Unfolding Johann Sebastian

01:11:43.870 --> 01:11:44.880
Bach.

01:11:44.880 --> 01:11:48.059
We all know Johann Sebastian
Bach as an influential composer

01:11:48.059 --> 01:11:51.510
that is still affecting
musical creation to this date,

01:11:51.510 --> 01:11:53.260
but have you thought of
Johann Sebastian Bach

01:11:53.260 --> 01:11:55.630
as an inventor of new proteins?

01:11:55.630 --> 01:11:58.639
As a creator of
new biochemistry?

01:11:58.639 --> 01:12:01.039
We have discovered that Bach
might have inadvertently

01:12:01.039 --> 01:12:04.479
created, designed, and
realized new forms of life,

01:12:04.479 --> 01:12:07.389
new forms of proteins,
new biomaterials

01:12:07.389 --> 01:12:09.820
that evolution has
not yet discovered,

01:12:09.820 --> 01:12:12.210
but they have been created
through the creation

01:12:12.210 --> 01:12:14.170
of musical art.

01:12:14.170 --> 01:12:17.590
This provides evidence for
hidden information created

01:12:17.590 --> 01:12:20.890
across time-scales, created
across cultural information

01:12:20.890 --> 01:12:24.199
levels, to inform science
today, and perhaps

01:12:24.199 --> 01:12:28.479
the future of understanding life
on this planet and elsewhere.

01:12:28.479 --> 01:12:29.749
When you think
about the formation

01:12:29.749 --> 01:12:32.600
of the fundamental
building blocks of life,

01:12:32.600 --> 01:12:34.659
you begin to think
about proteins.

01:12:34.659 --> 01:12:37.489
Proteins really are the
elementary ancient evolutionary

01:12:37.489 --> 01:12:41.489
memories of a very distant past
during which evolution yielded

01:12:41.489 --> 01:12:43.880
the kind of life we
have on Earth today

01:12:43.880 --> 01:12:47.550
and that emerged over millions
and billions of years.

01:12:47.550 --> 01:12:50.699
Proteins spontaneously
come together, or fold,

01:12:50.699 --> 01:12:53.960
at very short time-scales,
beginning with time-scales

01:12:53.960 --> 01:12:56.090
of 10 to the minus
15 seconds, which

01:12:56.090 --> 01:13:01.150
are the scales at which
molecular connections vibrate.

01:13:01.150 --> 01:13:02.949
On the other hand, on the
other side of the spectrum,

01:13:02.949 --> 01:13:08.250
we have human-created assemblies
of structures, such as music.

01:13:08.250 --> 01:13:11.690
Music is an assembly of pitches,
notes, different timbres

01:13:11.690 --> 01:13:14.190
of instruments in
complex arrangement,

01:13:14.190 --> 01:13:16.300
in complex, hierarchical
arrangements

01:13:16.300 --> 01:13:20.260
that span multiple
time and length-scales.

01:13:20.260 --> 01:13:21.989
Through the assembly
of notes, we

01:13:21.989 --> 01:13:24.599
can induce emotional
responses in our brain.

01:13:24.599 --> 01:13:27.510
Through music, we
can convey messages.

01:13:27.510 --> 01:13:29.900
Either direct messages,
obvious messages,

01:13:29.900 --> 01:13:33.110
or subliminal messages that
are underlying currents

01:13:33.110 --> 01:13:35.860
in the musical creation.

01:13:35.860 --> 01:13:37.960
With the new tools of
artificial intelligence,

01:13:37.960 --> 01:13:41.170
we can now make new
connections and discover

01:13:41.170 --> 01:13:43.679
hidden physical
realizations of information

01:13:43.679 --> 01:13:46.560
in art to advance science,
humanity, and culture.

01:13:46.560 --> 01:13:48.460
There are structural
forms in art

01:13:48.460 --> 01:13:51.820
that we believe can
only now be discovered

01:13:51.820 --> 01:13:53.540
using modern technologies.

01:13:53.540 --> 01:13:56.960
Our research deals with
multi-scale materials.

01:13:56.960 --> 01:13:59.199
We look at matter from a
multiple-scale-perspective.

01:13:59.199 --> 01:14:02.611
From the atomic scale, where
we have dimensions of 10

01:14:02.611 --> 01:14:04.719
to the minus 10
and smaller meters,

01:14:04.719 --> 01:14:06.699
which are the scales
of atoms, and orbitals,

01:14:06.699 --> 01:14:07.969
and chemical connections.

01:14:07.969 --> 01:14:09.809
And we go all the way
up to the macro level,

01:14:09.809 --> 01:14:11.849
where we have scales
at the human level

01:14:11.849 --> 01:14:14.590
of a couple of meters, or
at the scale of the planet,

01:14:14.590 --> 01:14:17.469
many, many thousands of
meters and kilometers,

01:14:17.469 --> 01:14:19.699
or to the scale of the universe,
where we have even much

01:14:19.699 --> 01:14:22.070
larger length scales.

01:14:22.070 --> 01:14:24.679
These materials can be looked
at through this microscope

01:14:24.679 --> 01:14:25.780
of different magnifications.

01:14:25.780 --> 01:14:27.530
And on each level
of magnification,

01:14:27.530 --> 01:14:31.539
we're going to see different
structural features.

01:14:31.539 --> 01:14:34.119
These structural features
range from the atomic scale

01:14:34.119 --> 01:14:37.440
through the micro/meso scale,
all the way to the macro scale.

01:14:37.440 --> 01:14:38.710
And materials derive
their functions--

01:14:38.710 --> 01:14:42.449
their unique properties such as
color, mechanical properties,

01:14:42.449 --> 01:14:45.369
optical properties,
magnetic properties,

01:14:45.369 --> 01:14:48.249
diffusive properties, and any
other kind of technologically

01:14:48.249 --> 01:14:50.630
important or culturally
important properties--

01:14:50.630 --> 01:14:53.070
through these features across
these different scales.

01:14:53.070 --> 01:14:55.940
These are patterns that
humans, in part, have created,

01:14:55.940 --> 01:15:01.400
that nature creates all the time
during evolutionary processes.

01:15:01.400 --> 01:15:03.130
One of the fundamental
processes we're interested in

01:15:03.130 --> 01:15:05.369
is the breaking of materials.

01:15:05.369 --> 01:15:06.960
Materials break in
characteristic time-scales

01:15:06.960 --> 01:15:08.949
of 10 to the minus 12 seconds.

01:15:08.949 --> 01:15:12.150
There's a universality
of vibrations and waves

01:15:12.150 --> 01:15:14.769
which occurs at every
observational level

01:15:14.769 --> 01:15:17.020
that we have
currently available.

01:15:17.020 --> 01:15:19.599
We have molecules which can be
understood based on their wave

01:15:19.599 --> 01:15:21.280
particle duality.

01:15:21.280 --> 01:15:24.230
Quantum scale objects behave
either as a wave or a particle

01:15:24.230 --> 01:15:26.139
simultaneously.

01:15:26.139 --> 01:15:27.479
We have sound waves, of course.

01:15:27.479 --> 01:15:28.550
That's how we communicate.

01:15:28.550 --> 01:15:30.539
That's how animals communicate.

01:15:30.539 --> 01:15:32.780
And we have used sounds
in the form of music

01:15:32.780 --> 01:15:34.521
as a cultural
expression, as a way

01:15:34.521 --> 01:15:37.599
of affecting memory and
passing on information

01:15:37.599 --> 01:15:40.199
through generations of cultures.

01:15:40.199 --> 01:15:43.489
And of course, we have the
insect world-- spiders, bees,

01:15:43.489 --> 01:15:46.369
and wasps all communicate
through vibrations.

01:15:46.369 --> 01:15:48.909
With tools we have now available
of artificial intelligence,

01:15:48.909 --> 01:15:50.340
molecular dynamics
modeling, we can

01:15:50.340 --> 01:15:53.789
map these different time-scales
on a single observational level

01:15:53.789 --> 01:15:55.550
and actually make
them understandable

01:15:55.550 --> 01:15:59.179
through new processes in
the use of the human brain.

01:15:59.179 --> 01:16:00.460
In the beginning
of the universe,

01:16:00.460 --> 01:16:03.940
we had a fairly even
distribution of matter.

01:16:03.940 --> 01:16:05.889
Small fluctuations
of matter ultimately

01:16:05.889 --> 01:16:09.350
led to the creation of planets,
galaxies, and all the things

01:16:09.350 --> 01:16:12.639
we have today, including
atoms, of course.

01:16:12.639 --> 01:16:14.340
Localization of
information, localization

01:16:14.340 --> 01:16:17.130
of energy or matter
which are equivalent,

01:16:17.130 --> 01:16:21.440
leads to the creation of
matter as we know it today.

01:16:21.440 --> 01:16:23.110
In a very similar
way, when we look

01:16:23.110 --> 01:16:25.420
at spiders as an
animal that creates

01:16:25.420 --> 01:16:28.039
an extra-organismal
structure, the spider web,

01:16:28.039 --> 01:16:30.329
it also does this using
a very similar mechanism.

01:16:30.329 --> 01:16:32.489
A spider might eat a fly.

01:16:32.489 --> 01:16:36.249
The spider's world consists
of eating things, processing

01:16:36.249 --> 01:16:40.329
the things it has eaten
in the body of the spider,

01:16:40.329 --> 01:16:42.679
and then reassembling these
building blocks of what

01:16:42.679 --> 01:16:44.760
it has eaten into a structure.

01:16:44.760 --> 01:16:48.300
From a distribution-- a
random distribution of protein

01:16:48.300 --> 01:16:51.330
building blocks that the
spider has digested from eating

01:16:51.330 --> 01:16:52.330
a fly--

01:16:52.330 --> 01:16:55.110
it can create beautiful
structures, as shown here.

01:16:55.110 --> 01:16:57.389
And at the foundation of these
web structures, of course,

01:16:57.389 --> 01:16:58.389
are these protein molecules.

01:16:58.389 --> 01:17:01.949
And these protein molecules
are made by themselves

01:17:01.949 --> 01:17:05.659
from what we call amino acids,
or ultimately encoded by DNA.

01:17:05.659 --> 01:17:07.610
And these can be viewed
as a LEGO building block.

01:17:07.610 --> 01:17:09.999
So when the spider eats
a fly, it essentially

01:17:09.999 --> 01:17:13.170
ingests a very large number
of LEGO building blocks,

01:17:13.170 --> 01:17:16.039
and it has a random collection
of these in its body.

01:17:16.039 --> 01:17:18.260
And the spider has
found an ingenious way

01:17:18.260 --> 01:17:20.409
to then reassemble these
building blocks, these LEGO

01:17:20.409 --> 01:17:22.559
building blocks,
into a structure.

01:17:22.559 --> 01:17:24.809
To organize them in
space and in time

01:17:24.809 --> 01:17:27.699
to create a certain material
function, which in this case,

01:17:27.699 --> 01:17:29.300
is the web's function--
one of the functions--

01:17:29.300 --> 01:17:33.400
is to catch more prey for
the spider to survive.

01:17:33.400 --> 01:17:35.760
Here's the result of an
experimental analysis

01:17:35.760 --> 01:17:38.350
where we observe the
spider building a web.

01:17:38.350 --> 01:17:41.969
This is work in collaboration
with our cast collaborator,

01:17:41.969 --> 01:17:45.079
Tomas Saraceno, with whom
we watched the spider build

01:17:45.079 --> 01:17:47.280
a web over a period of days.

01:17:47.280 --> 01:17:49.159
And you can see how
the spider is truly

01:17:49.159 --> 01:17:52.280
an autonomous 3D printer,
depositing materials in space,

01:17:52.280 --> 01:17:54.619
choosing the location,
and thereby creating

01:17:54.619 --> 01:17:57.320
a hierarchical structure
that is both aesthetically

01:17:57.320 --> 01:18:00.480
beautiful, but also, more
importantly for the spider,

01:18:00.480 --> 01:18:01.480
functional.

01:18:01.480 --> 01:18:05.510
And in a similar way, when life
emerged billions of years ago--

01:18:05.510 --> 01:18:07.650
we're looking now at the
evolutionary time-scales--

01:18:07.650 --> 01:18:10.099
we had random processes
and random fluctuations

01:18:10.099 --> 01:18:11.260
that created structure.

01:18:11.260 --> 01:18:13.429
The basic structure of
the formation of RNA

01:18:13.429 --> 01:18:16.199
or other molecules which
ultimately created life

01:18:16.199 --> 01:18:20.260
as we know it today, built from
DNA, proteins, and other kinds

01:18:20.260 --> 01:18:21.260
of biomolecules.

01:18:21.260 --> 01:18:24.039
And this led to the
creation of all sorts

01:18:24.039 --> 01:18:26.019
of different organisms,
as we know today.

01:18:26.019 --> 01:18:29.380
And again, these organisms have
evolved because ultimately,

01:18:29.380 --> 01:18:32.260
at the basic level,
molecules assembled

01:18:32.260 --> 01:18:33.523
in particular structure
from a random distribution

01:18:33.523 --> 01:18:37.449
of molecules, a barren
landscape that this planet

01:18:37.449 --> 01:18:41.530
was billions of years ago
where then suddenly, molecules

01:18:41.530 --> 01:18:43.949
began to arrange,
became living objects,

01:18:43.949 --> 01:18:45.690
living subjects,
which ultimately

01:18:45.690 --> 01:18:48.699
led to the creation of the
human race as we know it today.

01:18:48.699 --> 01:18:51.429
Music, in a very similar
way, is an election

01:18:51.429 --> 01:18:52.889
of localization of energy.

01:18:52.889 --> 01:18:55.050
This election is
created, for example,

01:18:55.050 --> 01:18:58.749
through the creative mind or
other processes or animals

01:18:58.749 --> 01:19:02.300
which use sound or
music to communicate.

01:19:02.300 --> 01:19:04.880
White noise is the
equal distribution

01:19:04.880 --> 01:19:06.989
of tonal energy at
all frequencies,

01:19:06.989 --> 01:19:09.480
creating a chaotic
and random structure.

01:19:09.480 --> 01:19:13.670
And we represent this in our
ear and our brains as noise.

01:19:13.670 --> 01:19:15.329
[WHITE NOISE]

01:19:15.329 --> 01:19:20.639
This is in stark contrast to
what we identify as harmony,

01:19:20.639 --> 01:19:24.010
where instead of having a random
distribution of information

01:19:24.010 --> 01:19:26.809
on all frequencies, we now
have a distribution at very

01:19:26.809 --> 01:19:28.550
particular frequencies.

01:19:28.550 --> 01:19:30.800
By playing tones at very
particular frequencies

01:19:30.800 --> 01:19:32.590
or a selection of
frequencies, we

01:19:32.590 --> 01:19:35.350
can create musical information,
and encode information,

01:19:35.350 --> 01:19:37.070
and create an
impression in our brain

01:19:37.070 --> 01:19:40.889
that causes an
emotional response.

01:19:40.889 --> 01:19:45.860
[PIANO MUSIC]

01:19:45.860 --> 01:19:48.829
Here's an example where I
begin playing white noise

01:19:48.829 --> 01:19:51.920
and then use this white noise
as a basis to create sound

01:19:51.920 --> 01:19:54.579
that we realize in
our brain as music.

01:19:54.579 --> 01:19:58.239
I use the white noise and then
filter out only the harmonics.

01:19:58.239 --> 01:20:00.440
These harmonics are
the building blocks

01:20:00.440 --> 01:20:02.300
for very simple forms of music.

01:20:02.300 --> 01:20:04.300
And you can hear as
the sound progresses

01:20:04.300 --> 01:20:06.519
we go from white
noise, filtering out

01:20:06.519 --> 01:20:08.520
information and only
localizing energy

01:20:08.520 --> 01:20:11.499
at these harmonic frequencies
into a single tone.

01:20:11.499 --> 01:20:14.190
We can then play music
in the form of octaves

01:20:14.190 --> 01:20:16.190
by using equal
temperament tuning,

01:20:16.190 --> 01:20:18.110
and even create
chord progressions

01:20:18.110 --> 01:20:19.739
or other, more complex sounds.

01:20:19.739 --> 01:20:22.349
And as we turn
the filter off, we

01:20:22.349 --> 01:20:23.920
return back to the white noise.

01:20:23.920 --> 01:20:26.929
So in this case, we have
created a random structure

01:20:26.929 --> 01:20:28.330
and we have localized
information,

01:20:28.330 --> 01:20:31.080
we have localized energy and
created what we call music,

01:20:31.080 --> 01:20:48.880
or one of the forms of
music that we know as today.

01:20:48.880 --> 01:20:55.500
[WHITE NOISE]

01:20:55.500 --> 01:20:58.820
[BUZZING]

01:20:58.820 --> 01:21:05.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:21:05.440 --> 01:21:12.070
[WHITE NOISE]

01:21:12.070 --> 01:21:15.199
Another way to create music
is to start from silence,

01:21:15.199 --> 01:21:18.110
and only create
tones or a sound that

01:21:18.110 --> 01:21:22.650
reflects certain types of
frequencies or vibrations.

01:21:22.650 --> 01:21:23.929
This is a localization
of frequencies

01:21:23.929 --> 01:21:26.269
that we typically use
in the conventional way

01:21:26.269 --> 01:21:29.630
of using analog instruments,
like a vibrating string.

01:21:29.630 --> 01:21:31.719
These vibrating strings vibrate
at very particular frequencies.

01:21:31.719 --> 01:21:32.719
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:21:32.719 --> 01:21:34.469
We can tune them by changing
the tension or the length

01:21:34.469 --> 01:21:36.769
of these strings, and
we can then create music

01:21:36.769 --> 01:21:39.369
by having different
types of tuning systems,

01:21:39.369 --> 01:21:42.090
and plucking the strings
differently or bowing them

01:21:42.090 --> 01:21:43.090
differently.

01:21:43.090 --> 01:21:44.989
And of course, an
example for highlighting

01:21:44.989 --> 01:21:48.030
the emergence of sound
or music from silence

01:21:48.030 --> 01:21:50.960
is the famous piece, 4'33.

01:21:50.960 --> 01:21:53.030
The strings don't
have to necessarily

01:21:53.030 --> 01:21:54.320
be conventionally tuned.

01:21:54.320 --> 01:21:58.200
They don't have to look like
in the guitar, or in a piano,

01:21:58.200 --> 01:22:00.389
or perhaps in a violin,
a viola, or cello.

01:22:00.389 --> 01:22:02.849
These strings actually
can also come from nature.

01:22:02.849 --> 01:22:06.510
And in some recent work,
we have used spiderwebs,

01:22:06.510 --> 01:22:10.099
and used the individual strings
in spiderwebs as musical form,

01:22:10.099 --> 01:22:12.119
as musical creation process.

01:22:12.119 --> 01:22:14.369
And you can imagine that
these strings in a spiderweb

01:22:14.369 --> 01:22:16.780
are not tuned according to
any of the tuning system

01:22:16.780 --> 01:22:17.969
we're usually familiar with.

01:22:17.969 --> 01:22:19.909
In fact they're
tuned by the spider

01:22:19.909 --> 01:22:23.510
to follow certain rules and
laws that the spider uses

01:22:23.510 --> 01:22:25.739
to communicate, but we
can make them audible.

01:22:25.739 --> 01:22:26.949
And what you hear
in the background

01:22:26.949 --> 01:22:29.760
is an example of how these
types of vibrations sound like.

01:22:29.760 --> 01:22:31.079
It's a strange
world, but you can

01:22:31.079 --> 01:22:33.119
begin to live in the
world of the spider

01:22:33.119 --> 01:22:35.119
and engage in the
process of feeling

01:22:35.119 --> 01:23:01.729
a little bit like

01:23:01.729 --> 01:23:05.039
a spider.

01:23:05.039 --> 01:23:11.679
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:23:11.679 --> 01:23:13.560
Music changes the
state of mind and it

01:23:13.560 --> 01:23:14.920
has affected our physicality.

01:23:14.920 --> 01:23:17.219
So when you listen to
music, when you hear sounds,

01:23:17.219 --> 01:23:18.709
you have a response
in your brain.

01:23:18.709 --> 01:23:20.559
It's a physical, real response.

01:23:20.559 --> 01:23:22.139
Things are changing
in your brain.

01:23:22.139 --> 01:23:23.360
Things are changing.

01:23:23.360 --> 01:23:26.429
Material changes are happening
as you listen to music.

01:23:26.429 --> 01:23:27.510
Before and after is different.

01:23:27.510 --> 01:23:31.309
Our question now is, can
these material changes

01:23:31.309 --> 01:23:34.170
yield actual new material form?

01:23:34.170 --> 01:23:35.630
Instead of changing our brain--

01:23:35.630 --> 01:23:37.579
state of the molecules in
our brain, the chemicals

01:23:37.579 --> 01:23:39.659
in our brain, the
cells, the neurons--

01:23:39.659 --> 01:23:43.690
can we utilize music as a way
of creating new material form,

01:23:43.690 --> 01:23:46.340
or perhaps new life forms?

01:23:46.340 --> 01:23:48.690
For example, can we
create new biomaterials?

01:23:48.690 --> 01:23:50.900
Can we create new
types of cells?

01:23:50.900 --> 01:23:52.360
Can we create new proteins?

01:23:52.360 --> 01:23:54.389
This has been the
topic of my research

01:23:54.389 --> 01:23:55.960
in the last couple of years.

01:23:55.960 --> 01:23:57.539
And we asked the
question, how can we

01:23:57.539 --> 01:24:00.820
bring resonances and motions
from the distant past--

01:24:00.820 --> 01:24:03.380
perhaps from composers that
have composed music, created

01:24:03.380 --> 01:24:05.780
music hundreds, maybe
even thousands of years

01:24:05.780 --> 01:24:07.409
ago-- to the future?

01:24:07.409 --> 01:24:10.130
And how can we analyze them,
understand them, and actually

01:24:10.130 --> 01:24:13.920
use them to create new kinds
of material expressions

01:24:13.920 --> 01:24:16.150
and extract information
that's not obvious--

01:24:16.150 --> 01:24:17.889
subliminal information
that's hidden

01:24:17.889 --> 01:24:19.619
in the musical structure?

01:24:19.619 --> 01:24:22.400
We can use this approach to
better understand materials.

01:24:22.400 --> 01:24:24.750
We can use it to define
new musical forms,

01:24:24.750 --> 01:24:27.309
as a new way of composing
music, of creating music.

01:24:27.309 --> 01:24:29.139
And we can also learn
about ourselves,

01:24:29.139 --> 01:24:31.710
because this music
is created by humans.

01:24:31.710 --> 01:24:34.030
And by studying
what humans create

01:24:34.030 --> 01:24:36.340
and what hidden messages
we might be sending,

01:24:36.340 --> 01:24:37.800
we can communicate
across generations.

01:24:37.800 --> 01:24:40.969
So again, you have a
multitude of length-scales

01:24:40.969 --> 01:24:45.820
and time-scales involved
here in this process.

01:24:45.820 --> 01:24:47.929
Proteins are the
hardware of life,

01:24:47.929 --> 01:24:49.389
and introduction of
proteins essentially

01:24:49.389 --> 01:24:51.460
begins with the level of DNA.

01:24:51.460 --> 01:24:54.039
DNA is essentially
the coding structure.

01:24:54.039 --> 01:24:56.869
It's elementary to
understand how life is built.

01:24:56.869 --> 01:24:58.599
It's the building plan for that.

01:24:58.599 --> 01:25:01.440
DNA encodes the way
proteins are actually

01:25:01.440 --> 01:25:04.940
physically constructed, how
the amino acids in proteins

01:25:04.940 --> 01:25:06.590
are put together.

01:25:06.590 --> 01:25:09.199
The protein folds
at these micro-millisecond

01:25:09.199 --> 01:25:12.710
time-scales to form,
ultimately, living subjects.

01:25:12.710 --> 01:25:14.679
And these living
subjects have functions.

01:25:14.679 --> 01:25:16.550
They form, perhaps,
cells in a body.

01:25:16.550 --> 01:25:18.579
They form organisms, organs.

01:25:18.579 --> 01:25:21.179
That can form tissues--

01:25:21.179 --> 01:25:23.610
our skin, you can
think about your eyes.

01:25:23.610 --> 01:25:26.159
All of these things are really,
truly, made from proteins.

01:25:26.159 --> 01:25:28.820
So proteins are the workhorse,
the actual materialization

01:25:28.820 --> 01:25:30.219
of life.

01:25:30.219 --> 01:25:33.219
And in the evolution, this
plays a very important role.

01:25:33.219 --> 01:25:34.739
Proteins are
evolving, of course,

01:25:34.739 --> 01:25:37.479
through genetic mutations,
through natural selection,

01:25:37.479 --> 01:25:39.510
and these processes lead
to different life forms.

01:25:39.510 --> 01:25:40.660
So all these
different life forms

01:25:40.660 --> 01:25:42.809
have different
kinds of proteins.

01:25:42.809 --> 01:25:45.099
Some proteins have survived
evolutionary processes,

01:25:45.099 --> 01:25:47.739
and are found across
different species.

01:25:47.739 --> 01:25:51.329
Other types of protein molecules
are only found in one species,

01:25:51.329 --> 01:25:54.170
and maybe in one particular
niche, ecologically.

01:25:54.170 --> 01:25:55.749
Now, how do proteins look like?

01:25:55.749 --> 01:25:57.369
When you open the
textbook and you

01:25:57.369 --> 01:25:58.940
look into molecular
structures, you're

01:25:58.940 --> 01:26:00.739
going to find a
picture like this.

01:26:00.739 --> 01:26:02.230
And you're going to find
a chemical structure--

01:26:02.230 --> 01:26:05.679
usually out of water molecules,
maybe some metal ions--

01:26:05.679 --> 01:26:08.850
and really reflecting a
complex folded geometry,

01:26:08.850 --> 01:26:11.579
and thereby deriving
chemical, biochemical,

01:26:11.579 --> 01:26:13.099
and evolutionary functions.

01:26:13.099 --> 01:26:16.329
But if you think about proteins
and molecules in general,

01:26:16.329 --> 01:26:17.519
that's really not
how they look like.

01:26:17.519 --> 01:26:18.860
If you take a real
microscope and you

01:26:18.860 --> 01:26:21.219
watch the molecular motions,
which now we can actually

01:26:21.219 --> 01:26:23.050
image experimentally,
you can see

01:26:23.050 --> 01:26:25.920
that these atoms and molecules
are actually continuously

01:26:25.920 --> 01:26:29.179
moving and vibrating.

01:26:29.179 --> 01:26:31.719
As these molecules
continuously vibrate and move,

01:26:31.719 --> 01:26:34.360
they follow equations of
motions which ultimately are

01:26:34.360 --> 01:26:36.610
governed by quantum chemistry.

01:26:36.610 --> 01:26:38.539
These quantum
chemical laws can be

01:26:38.539 --> 01:26:40.889
implemented in an algorithm
called molecular dynamics.

01:26:40.889 --> 01:26:43.489
And we can actually watch,
simulate, and observe

01:26:43.489 --> 01:26:45.179
the motions of these molecules.

01:26:45.179 --> 01:26:47.760
These vibrations happen
on the scale of 10

01:26:47.760 --> 01:26:50.080
to the minus 15
seconds and higher.

01:26:50.080 --> 01:26:51.599
So these are very,
very short time-scales

01:26:51.599 --> 01:26:54.099
compared to the scale of
observation of the human mind,

01:26:54.099 --> 01:26:55.099
for instance.

01:26:55.099 --> 01:26:58.040
And if you look at a
simulation of a protein--

01:26:58.040 --> 01:26:59.590
this is an alpha
helical protein--

01:26:59.590 --> 01:27:02.090
you can see that these
vibrations, jittering motions,

01:27:02.090 --> 01:27:04.829
are quite interesting, quite
beautiful, very elegant.

01:27:04.829 --> 01:27:07.000
And you can see they have
certain patterns with them.

01:27:07.000 --> 01:27:08.459
And these patterns
are associated

01:27:08.459 --> 01:27:10.320
with certain
structural features.

01:27:10.320 --> 01:27:12.420
We can distinguish
different kinds of proteins

01:27:12.420 --> 01:27:14.500
by watching different kinds
of features or patterns

01:27:14.500 --> 01:27:17.550
emerge at these very,
very short time-scales.

01:27:17.550 --> 01:27:19.880
Now, these
vibrational signatures

01:27:19.880 --> 01:27:21.810
are the elementary
description of life.

01:27:21.810 --> 01:27:25.090
Proteins are made from 20
naturally-occurring amino

01:27:25.090 --> 01:27:26.090
acids.

01:27:26.090 --> 01:27:28.380
And each of these 20 amino
acids, we've discovered,

01:27:28.380 --> 01:27:30.209
has a unique
vibrational signature.

01:27:30.209 --> 01:27:32.719
They vibrate in different
forms, in different ways.

01:27:32.719 --> 01:27:34.179
And these vibrational
signatures are

01:27:34.179 --> 01:27:36.309
sort of bar codes for
the elementary building

01:27:36.309 --> 01:27:38.510
blocks of life.

01:27:38.510 --> 01:27:40.719
And this represents a
localization of energy.

01:27:40.719 --> 01:27:42.900
Just as we talked about
it during the creation

01:27:42.900 --> 01:27:45.479
of the universe a
very long time ago,

01:27:45.479 --> 01:27:48.059
during the emergence of life,
during the creation of music

01:27:48.059 --> 01:27:50.729
through the human creative
mind, in all of these processes

01:27:50.729 --> 01:27:52.309
there's a common
thread, a common theme

01:27:52.309 --> 01:27:54.199
that we localize structure.

01:27:54.199 --> 01:27:55.869
We put things in
a certain place.

01:27:55.869 --> 01:27:57.390
We move things at
the other place,

01:27:57.390 --> 01:27:59.390
or we don't even put them
there at the beginning.

01:27:59.390 --> 01:28:02.260
And thereby, we create a
distribution, a patterning.

01:28:02.260 --> 01:28:04.340
And the way life emerges
is creating patterns

01:28:04.340 --> 01:28:06.000
across these 20 amino acids.

01:28:06.000 --> 01:28:09.139
Now, these 20 amino acids
can be made audible.

01:28:09.139 --> 01:28:12.050
This information is stored
at very high frequencies,

01:28:12.050 --> 01:28:13.280
and naturally, we
cannot hear them.

01:28:13.280 --> 01:28:17.880
Our auditory system can only
hear up to about 20 kilohertz.

01:28:17.880 --> 01:28:19.260
We can solve this
problem by using

01:28:19.260 --> 01:28:23.579
the concept of transposition,
or transpositional equivalence.

01:28:23.579 --> 01:28:25.409
As this simple
example shows, I'm

01:28:25.409 --> 01:28:27.929
beginning to play a melody
in the beginning at very

01:28:27.929 --> 01:28:30.820
high frequencies, and
I'm slowly moving down.

01:28:30.820 --> 01:28:33.289
I'm transposing
this piece of music

01:28:33.289 --> 01:28:36.079
into lower and lower frequencies
until it becomes audible.

01:28:36.079 --> 01:28:39.360
So as you listen, pay close
attention and see at what point

01:28:39.360 --> 01:28:47.440
you actually recognize
what melody I'm playing.

01:28:47.440 --> 01:28:59.849
[TONES SOUNDING]

01:28:59.849 --> 01:29:24.659
[MUSIC - BEETHOVEN, "FUR ELISE"]

01:29:24.659 --> 01:30:02.909
Once you have recognized the
melody, I'm playing it again.

01:30:02.909 --> 01:30:05.739
I'm transposing it again
to lower frequencies.

01:30:05.739 --> 01:30:07.650
And to your ear,
to your brain, you

01:30:07.650 --> 01:30:11.000
can clearly still identify that
this is the same melody-- it's

01:30:11.000 --> 01:30:12.659
Fur Elise by Beethoven.

01:30:12.659 --> 01:30:15.659
However, I'm playing it
at yet lower frequencies.

01:30:15.659 --> 01:30:17.670
And this is what we call
transpositional equivalence.

01:30:17.670 --> 01:30:20.340
We can play melodies at
different frequency levels

01:30:20.340 --> 01:30:23.750
by keeping the ratios, the
multipliers of frequencies,

01:30:23.750 --> 01:30:24.750
the same.

01:30:24.750 --> 01:30:26.869
And thereby, we retain
musical information

01:30:26.869 --> 01:30:30.239
or structural information that
is elementary to understanding,

01:30:30.239 --> 01:30:34.050
through the auditory system in
our brain, what kind of sound

01:30:34.050 --> 01:30:35.869
has been created
and what that means

01:30:35.869 --> 01:30:39.092
for our processing in the brain.

01:30:39.092 --> 01:30:42.449
In a similar way, we can use
this to translate proteins

01:30:42.449 --> 01:30:43.449
into audible spectrum.

01:30:43.449 --> 01:30:44.449
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:30:44.449 --> 01:30:45.449
And what you hear
in the background

01:30:45.449 --> 01:30:47.190
now are all these 20
amino acid building

01:30:47.190 --> 01:30:48.670
blocks played in a
succession, and you

01:30:48.670 --> 01:30:51.239
can hear that each of these
20 amino acids building blocks

01:30:51.239 --> 01:30:53.179
has a unique tone
associated with it.

01:30:53.179 --> 01:30:56.800
It's kind of like having its
own key on a keyboard or piano.

01:30:56.800 --> 01:30:58.670
However, this is
not a typical piano

01:30:58.670 --> 01:31:01.039
because the tuning
system, of course, now,

01:31:01.039 --> 01:31:02.039
is a different one.

01:31:02.039 --> 01:31:04.280
It's a distinct tuning system
that we call the amino acid

01:31:04.280 --> 01:31:05.280
scale.

01:31:05.280 --> 01:31:06.940
It follows the quantum
mechanical laws

01:31:06.940 --> 01:31:09.829
by which these 20
naturally-occurring amino acids

01:31:09.829 --> 01:31:11.860
actually vibrate.

01:31:11.860 --> 01:31:13.739
We can thereby map
proteins to sound.

01:31:13.739 --> 01:31:15.449
What you hear in
this example here

01:31:15.449 --> 01:31:17.159
is the mapping of a
protein called lysozyme.

01:31:17.159 --> 01:31:18.159
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:31:18.159 --> 01:31:20.969
It's an enzyme that's been
studied for many decades.

01:31:20.969 --> 01:31:24.489
This protein has been translated
now through this process

01:31:24.489 --> 01:31:26.300
into a musical piece.

01:31:26.300 --> 01:31:27.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:31:27.300 --> 01:31:29.499
In this process, we also
recognize the folding

01:31:29.499 --> 01:31:32.269
of molecules by playing
melodies against each other,

01:31:32.269 --> 01:31:35.119
creating a sort of counterpoint
emergence, a counterpoint

01:31:35.119 --> 01:31:38.489
function that is very similar,
for example, to Brian Eno's

01:31:38.489 --> 01:31:39.489
work using tapes.

01:31:39.489 --> 01:31:42.550
So these tapes, you can imagine,
are the amino acid or DNA

01:31:42.550 --> 01:31:45.550
sequences that are folded
on top of one another,

01:31:45.550 --> 01:31:48.679
thereby forming more complex
musical form than just

01:31:48.679 --> 01:31:49.679
the sequence.

01:31:49.679 --> 01:31:50.929
And we also
incorporate, of course,

01:31:50.929 --> 01:31:52.270
higher-level
structural information

01:31:52.270 --> 01:31:55.500
such as the vibrations at higher
length-scales and time-scales,

01:31:55.500 --> 01:31:57.699
as well as secondary structures.

01:31:57.699 --> 01:32:00.570
These are the distinctions
between an alpha helix or beta

01:32:00.570 --> 01:32:01.570
helix.

01:32:01.570 --> 01:32:03.159
Each of these have unique
auditory signatures

01:32:03.159 --> 01:32:04.209
which can be made audible.

01:32:04.209 --> 01:32:07.900
So we're creating a symphony
of auditory information

01:32:07.900 --> 01:32:10.120
that encodes the
three-dimensional structure

01:32:10.120 --> 01:32:13.920
of a protein from the very basic
level of amino acid sequences

01:32:13.920 --> 01:32:17.599
all the way to the
structural, the folding scale.

01:32:17.599 --> 01:32:20.539
And this can be applied not
only to the amino acid scale,

01:32:20.539 --> 01:32:23.449
we can actually approximate
the amino acid scale using

01:32:23.449 --> 01:32:26.699
conventional tuning systems
such as equal temperament tuning

01:32:26.699 --> 01:32:29.539
and convert these types of
protein sounds, these kinds

01:32:29.539 --> 01:32:32.929
of protein music, into a form
of Western classical music

01:32:32.929 --> 01:32:34.510
and play them
using an orchestra.

01:32:34.510 --> 01:32:36.249
And we've done this
recently in a piece where

01:32:36.249 --> 01:32:40.670
we have sonified the antibody
to the COVID-19 pathogen that

01:32:40.670 --> 01:32:43.739
forms when you take a vaccine,
or that forms when you are

01:32:43.739 --> 01:32:45.209
naturally ill from this virus.

01:32:45.209 --> 01:32:46.920
Your body produces
these antibodies

01:32:46.920 --> 01:32:48.760
which interact
with the virus, and

01:32:48.760 --> 01:32:51.320
these vibrational
interactions, this system

01:32:51.320 --> 01:32:54.889
of vibrating atoms and molecules
that interact with the virus,

01:32:54.889 --> 01:32:57.360
with the antibody
trying to defeat it,

01:32:57.360 --> 01:32:59.219
is what you hear in the
following example, which

01:32:59.219 --> 01:33:01.570
is set in a classical
score reflecting

01:33:01.570 --> 01:33:04.519
the information in this
particular protein system.

01:33:04.519 --> 01:33:06.640
This piece has been
played by the Lindenbaum

01:33:06.640 --> 01:33:14.239
Orchestra in Korea.

01:33:14.239 --> 01:33:46.110
We have realized this in
an orchestral setting.

01:33:46.110 --> 01:33:56.820
[MUSIC - LINDENBAUM ORCHESTRA,

01:33:56.820 --> 01:34:03.969
["PROTEIN ANTIBODY"]

01:34:03.969 --> 01:34:07.800
Why are we interested in
translating proteins to music?

01:34:07.800 --> 01:34:10.170
Beyond the aesthetic
approach, beyond, maybe,

01:34:10.170 --> 01:34:11.369
as an interesting
way of creating

01:34:11.369 --> 01:34:13.530
new musical form, creating
new musical competition,

01:34:13.530 --> 01:34:17.260
to inspire us to create
interesting musical structures,

01:34:17.260 --> 01:34:19.340
we can also use this
to design new proteins.

01:34:19.340 --> 01:34:21.679
We can create training sets
for artificial intelligence

01:34:21.679 --> 01:34:24.350
algorithms from this process.

01:34:24.350 --> 01:34:26.190
Imagine having a large database
of proteins-- thousands,

01:34:26.190 --> 01:34:28.880
hundreds of thousands of
proteins-- which we can then

01:34:28.880 --> 01:34:30.300
translate into music.

01:34:30.300 --> 01:34:33.789
We can let an AI algorithm
listen to all of these proteins

01:34:33.789 --> 01:34:37.489
and let the AI
compose new music.

01:34:37.489 --> 01:34:39.780
This new music can
then be translated back

01:34:39.780 --> 01:34:40.780
into a protein structure.

01:34:40.780 --> 01:34:42.380
This approach has
been very successful,

01:34:42.380 --> 01:34:44.380
and we have used
this to actually make

01:34:44.380 --> 01:34:47.010
new proteins in the lab that
we've designed through music.

01:34:47.010 --> 01:34:50.750
I should mention that instead
of having AI create music,

01:34:50.750 --> 01:34:54.070
of course, we can also have a
human composer create music,

01:34:54.070 --> 01:34:56.659
and write it in
the same form or style

01:34:56.659 --> 01:34:58.139
as these proteins produce.

01:34:58.139 --> 01:34:59.369
So we can learn these processes.

01:34:59.369 --> 01:35:03.130
We can learn the patterns,
the rhythms, the folding,

01:35:03.130 --> 01:35:05.400
the interactions, the
counterpoint structures,

01:35:05.400 --> 01:35:08.480
and utilize these to
thereby create new proteins.

01:35:08.480 --> 01:35:11.959
But using AIs of course,
is a perhaps simpler way

01:35:11.959 --> 01:35:14.909
because it's not influenced,
not biased in any way

01:35:14.909 --> 01:35:16.449
by the kinds of
musical impressions

01:35:16.449 --> 01:35:18.820
that we all have by listening
to all the other music

01:35:18.820 --> 01:35:20.579
we've ever heard in our life.

01:35:20.579 --> 01:35:23.939
This is the way by
which we translate music

01:35:23.939 --> 01:35:26.130
into a new kind of protein
or new kind of material.

01:35:26.130 --> 01:35:30.019
We begin this process by
creating a musical score.

01:35:30.019 --> 01:35:31.769
These musical scores
include all the information

01:35:31.769 --> 01:35:35.079
about the notes, the volume, the
rhythms, and the counterpoint

01:35:35.079 --> 01:35:36.110
information as well.

01:35:36.110 --> 01:35:37.809
And then we translate
these sequences

01:35:37.809 --> 01:35:39.929
into a sequence of
DNA building blocks.

01:35:39.929 --> 01:35:43.739
This DNA sequence then
forms the basic instruction

01:35:43.739 --> 01:35:45.210
set for cells.

01:35:45.210 --> 01:35:47.099
In this case, we've
used E. coli cells

01:35:47.099 --> 01:35:49.769
to then express this protein.

01:35:49.769 --> 01:35:52.520
Then the cells make the
protein, and we can basically

01:35:52.520 --> 01:35:55.869
slice the cell open and
extract the protein,

01:35:55.869 --> 01:35:57.380
and purify it, and
put it in a test tube.

01:35:57.380 --> 01:35:58.949
And what you see
on the right hand side

01:35:58.949 --> 01:36:01.340
is how such a test
tube looks like,

01:36:01.340 --> 01:36:04.479
which includes this protein
that was designed purely based

01:36:04.479 --> 01:36:05.479
on music.

01:36:05.479 --> 01:36:07.210
Now, this protein is a, what
we call, de novo protein.

01:36:07.210 --> 01:36:08.630
It doesn't exist in nature.

01:36:08.630 --> 01:36:10.489
So through this
musical approach we

01:36:10.489 --> 01:36:13.059
have discovered, instead of
using evolution as a way,

01:36:13.059 --> 01:36:14.849
over a very long
time-scale, we've

01:36:14.849 --> 01:36:17.539
used in a very elegant
way, musical creation

01:36:17.539 --> 01:36:19.250
as a way of designing
and realizing

01:36:19.250 --> 01:36:20.920
and to form a new protein.

01:36:20.920 --> 01:36:23.199
So now we're going
to do an experiment.

01:36:23.199 --> 01:36:26.800
Let's listen to the beginning
of the aria in Bach's Goldberg

01:36:26.800 --> 01:36:27.800
variation.

01:36:27.800 --> 01:36:30.309
And the question is,
what kind of information

01:36:30.309 --> 01:36:33.400
is stored in this very
beautiful but very simple

01:36:33.400 --> 01:36:36.230
succession of notes, of rhythms?

01:36:36.230 --> 01:36:40.079
[MUSIC - ARIA IN BACH'S "GOLDBERG VARIATIONS"
]

01:36:40.079 --> 01:36:43.289
And by analyzing this music
that Bach wrote a couple hundred

01:36:43.289 --> 01:36:46.309
years ago, we can assign
each of the musical notes

01:36:46.309 --> 01:36:48.349
in the aria, the
beginning of the aria,

01:36:48.349 --> 01:36:50.670
into a succession of
DNA codes by assigning

01:36:50.670 --> 01:36:54.360
unique associations with these
vibrations of amino acids.

01:36:54.360 --> 01:36:57.960
This DNA code can then
be formed as a basis

01:36:57.960 --> 01:37:00.820
into a protein expression,
just like we've done before,

01:37:00.820 --> 01:37:02.840
in an experimental way, by
putting these amino acid

01:37:02.840 --> 01:37:05.210
sequences or DNA
sequences into a cell.

01:37:05.210 --> 01:37:07.340
In this case, we've put
this into a deep learning

01:37:07.340 --> 01:37:08.889
and machine learning algorithm.

01:37:08.889 --> 01:37:10.289
These machine
learning algorithms

01:37:10.289 --> 01:37:14.119
have the capacity to predict
how these amino acid building

01:37:14.119 --> 01:37:16.369
blocks fold, or
assemble, at these micro

01:37:16.369 --> 01:37:18.719
to millisecond time-scales
and form a material.

01:37:18.719 --> 01:37:20.869
And these algorithms can
also predict, of course,

01:37:20.869 --> 01:37:22.340
whether a material
will form or not.

01:37:22.340 --> 01:37:24.160
And that's a big
question, because some

01:37:24.160 --> 01:37:26.989
of the successions
of notes or rhythms

01:37:26.989 --> 01:37:28.289
will not ever form a protein.

01:37:28.289 --> 01:37:31.239
There are certain rules by
which these musical creations

01:37:31.239 --> 01:37:33.949
in this protein amino
scale must follow.

01:37:33.949 --> 01:37:36.409
And the experiment
here is many fold.

01:37:36.409 --> 01:37:38.469
One of them is,
did Bach actually

01:37:38.469 --> 01:37:41.570
inadvertently create a protein
that can actually be folded

01:37:41.570 --> 01:37:43.289
and is stable or not?

01:37:43.289 --> 01:37:45.459
And the algorithm has predicted
a very surprising result.

01:37:45.459 --> 01:37:48.510
So we used this deep learning
algorithm to fold the protein,

01:37:48.510 --> 01:37:53.389
and we discovered that, in fact,
the first 146 notes of the aria

01:37:53.389 --> 01:37:56.019
indeed lead to an actual
protein that is stable.

01:37:56.019 --> 01:37:57.459
And you see on the right-hand
side how it looks like.

01:37:57.459 --> 01:38:00.229
So we went from the
musical form that you

01:38:00.229 --> 01:38:03.119
could hear into a
letter of DNA sequences,

01:38:03.119 --> 01:38:05.961
protein amino acid sequences,
to ultimately, a protein

01:38:05.961 --> 01:38:09.530
structure that we have folded,
and you can now see and watch.

01:38:09.530 --> 01:38:14.209
Now, this protein is sort of
the realization of this music.

01:38:14.209 --> 01:38:15.820
So I have on this
slide a comparison

01:38:15.820 --> 01:38:17.310
of the musical score
on the very left--

01:38:17.310 --> 01:38:19.060
the original musical score.

01:38:19.060 --> 01:38:20.850
And the right-hand side,
you see the protein.

01:38:20.850 --> 01:38:22.630
You can see again
in this example

01:38:22.630 --> 01:38:24.889
here that the
protein isn't static.

01:38:24.889 --> 01:38:26.439
As I mentioned at
the very beginning,

01:38:26.439 --> 01:38:28.189
a protein like this
is continuously

01:38:28.189 --> 01:38:29.289
moving and vibrating, right?

01:38:29.289 --> 01:38:32.079
So these atoms are
not sitting still.

01:38:32.079 --> 01:38:33.380
They are like mini strings--

01:38:33.380 --> 01:38:36.920
mini meaning at the scale of
10 to the minus 10 meters,

01:38:36.920 --> 01:38:39.880
so very, very tiny strings
or connections between atoms,

01:38:39.880 --> 01:38:41.059
the chemical bonds.

01:38:41.059 --> 01:38:43.530
They vibrate, and they
have a certain signature--

01:38:43.530 --> 01:38:44.679
a certain vibrational signature.

01:38:44.679 --> 01:38:46.800
Now what we've done
on this experiment,

01:38:46.800 --> 01:38:48.300
we've taken this
protein that we've

01:38:48.300 --> 01:38:50.860
created based on
Bach's composition

01:38:50.860 --> 01:38:53.820
and translated this
protein again into music.

01:38:53.820 --> 01:38:56.780
So the question is, can
we sonify this protein?

01:38:56.780 --> 01:38:59.469
Essentially, instead of taking
an existing protein that

01:38:59.469 --> 01:39:02.239
evolution has produced as I'd
done before-- the lysozyme,

01:39:02.239 --> 01:39:03.420
the enzyme--

01:39:03.420 --> 01:39:08.070
we ask, now, the question, can
we convert this protein created

01:39:08.070 --> 01:39:09.939
by Bach, inadvertently
of course,

01:39:09.939 --> 01:39:13.780
into a new musical structure,
and how would that sound like?

01:39:13.780 --> 01:39:16.150
Here we're going to
listen to that piece now.

01:39:16.150 --> 01:39:18.239
And what you hear
in this example

01:39:18.239 --> 01:39:21.219
is the protein that was folded
based on Bach's Goldberg

01:39:21.219 --> 01:39:24.280
Variation aria, the
first 146 notes including

01:39:24.280 --> 01:39:26.380
all the information about
how the protein folds,

01:39:26.380 --> 01:39:29.070
the secondary structures of
rhythm, of counterpoints.

01:39:29.070 --> 01:39:31.340
You can see the counterpoint
map on the right-hand side.

01:39:31.340 --> 01:39:33.949
And we can see what kind
of interesting new music

01:39:33.949 --> 01:39:39.860
comes out of that process.

01:39:39.860 --> 01:39:53.949
[DEEP ARIA PLAYING]

01:39:53.949 --> 01:39:59.750
So what this
experiment has shown

01:39:59.750 --> 01:40:04.329
is that the music that we have
created has new information.

01:40:04.329 --> 01:40:07.369
And the new information has been
added by using a deep learning

01:40:07.369 --> 01:40:10.969
algorithm to really
incorporate, truly,

01:40:10.969 --> 01:40:11.990
the ancient memory of evolution.

01:40:11.990 --> 01:40:14.389
The ancient memory of
evolution is essentially

01:40:14.389 --> 01:40:16.219
the building blocks,
the laws, by which

01:40:16.219 --> 01:40:18.719
proteins fold from a sequence.

01:40:18.719 --> 01:40:22.880
And this folding, this chemical
information, this basic life

01:40:22.880 --> 01:40:25.039
information, the living
system information,

01:40:25.039 --> 01:40:27.989
has now been incorporated
into this note sequence

01:40:27.989 --> 01:40:30.329
that Bach created,
and thereby created

01:40:30.329 --> 01:40:33.000
new musical information,
new sound information that

01:40:33.000 --> 01:40:34.019
was not there before.

01:40:34.019 --> 01:40:35.840
It actually has
created a piece that

01:40:35.840 --> 01:40:38.739
is about five minutes
long, in this case.

01:40:38.739 --> 01:40:40.651
This new protein is different
from any protein that

01:40:40.651 --> 01:40:42.050
has been created by evolution.

01:40:42.050 --> 01:40:43.849
In fact, we have
checked this protein

01:40:43.849 --> 01:40:47.239
against any known databases of
proteins and it doesn't exist.

01:40:47.239 --> 01:40:49.989
So Bach has actually created,
through this process of writing

01:40:49.989 --> 01:40:51.750
down notes, new proteins.

01:40:51.750 --> 01:40:53.849
We can now make this
protein in a laboratory

01:40:53.849 --> 01:40:55.769
and analyze what kind
of function it has.

01:40:55.769 --> 01:40:58.719
Now, the function it might
have, it could be an enzyme.

01:40:58.719 --> 01:40:59.719
It could be a
structural material.

01:40:59.719 --> 01:41:02.239
In other words, it could be
a strong and tough material,

01:41:02.239 --> 01:41:04.019
like in hair or fingernails.

01:41:04.019 --> 01:41:06.469
It could be a virus.

01:41:06.469 --> 01:41:07.770
It could be a pathogen.

01:41:07.770 --> 01:41:09.579
It could be a poison.

01:41:09.579 --> 01:41:11.209
It could be a hormone protein.

01:41:11.209 --> 01:41:12.209
We don't know what it is.

01:41:12.209 --> 01:41:14.749
And this is where, of
course, much further research

01:41:14.749 --> 01:41:17.889
is needed to understand the
exact function of this protein

01:41:17.889 --> 01:41:21.920
encoded by Bach.

01:41:21.920 --> 01:41:46.599
[MUSIC, "PROTEIN ENCODED IN BACH'S GOLDBERG
VARIATION"]

01:41:46.599 --> 01:41:58.219
But more broadly,
this process has also

01:41:58.219 --> 01:42:00.520
enabled us to create a new
way of composing music.

01:42:00.520 --> 01:42:02.550
So instead of
writing down notes,

01:42:02.550 --> 01:42:07.349
we can write down notes and then
ask a deep learning algorithm

01:42:07.349 --> 01:42:10.380
to incorporate new
information, new secret

01:42:10.380 --> 01:42:12.260
or hidden information
in this process.

01:42:12.260 --> 01:42:15.260
And through this process, we
can incorporate information

01:42:15.260 --> 01:42:18.320
across very, very long
time-scales and length-scales,

01:42:18.320 --> 01:42:20.539
essentially looking at the
evolutionary time-scales

01:42:20.539 --> 01:42:23.760
during which life and all
the proteins have evolved.

01:42:23.760 --> 01:42:26.110
And this creates a new
set of musical ideas

01:42:26.110 --> 01:42:28.749
that we can utilize, perhaps in
composition or in the writing

01:42:28.749 --> 01:42:31.459
of future musical forms.

01:42:31.459 --> 01:42:33.679
So there are a couple of things
we have understood, or learned

01:42:33.679 --> 01:42:35.459
from this experiment.

01:42:35.459 --> 01:42:39.420
One question you could ask, why
isn't the music that comes out

01:42:39.420 --> 01:42:42.110
of sonifying the protein that
we have created from Bach

01:42:42.110 --> 01:42:43.110
the same as in the beginning?

01:42:43.110 --> 01:42:45.209
And the answer is
that, of course,

01:42:45.209 --> 01:42:47.670
the folding process has
added new information.

01:42:47.670 --> 01:42:50.769
So we have utilized a different
medium, a different expression

01:42:50.769 --> 01:42:52.920
of information-- the
quantum chemistry,

01:42:52.920 --> 01:42:55.809
the evolutionary time-scales--
and added this information

01:42:55.809 --> 01:42:58.989
into the folding, and ultimately
into the listening experience.

01:42:58.989 --> 01:43:02.650
And so the music has
changed after the expression

01:43:02.650 --> 01:43:04.300
as a protein from
the DNA sequence

01:43:04.300 --> 01:43:06.460
that Bach originally invented.

01:43:06.460 --> 01:43:09.050
And this is a passage
of information

01:43:09.050 --> 01:43:13.059
through a very different
time-scale horizon,

01:43:13.059 --> 01:43:14.889
of course, that includes the
vibrations of the atoms which

01:43:14.889 --> 01:43:17.650
ultimately make the
sound, the music you hear.

01:43:17.650 --> 01:43:20.060
But the memory that we have
incorporated, of course,

01:43:20.060 --> 01:43:22.809
comes from a very,
very distant past.

01:43:22.809 --> 01:43:26.769
So AI can help us to create
art, or in particular music,

01:43:26.769 --> 01:43:29.070
as we've talked about
here in this presentation.

01:43:29.070 --> 01:43:30.750
But it really does
not replace humans.

01:43:30.750 --> 01:43:34.849
It can be a tool for humans
to become more creative

01:43:34.849 --> 01:43:38.590
or to get information and
answers from a distant past.

01:43:38.590 --> 01:43:42.369
We like to use proteins as a
way of gaining information,

01:43:42.369 --> 01:43:44.849
musical ideas, about
things that have happened

01:43:44.849 --> 01:43:45.959
in evolutionary time-scales.

01:43:45.959 --> 01:43:49.229
We like to interrogate
proteins and molecules

01:43:49.229 --> 01:43:52.159
to see or to hear
what is happening

01:43:52.159 --> 01:43:55.269
at the scale of vibrations of
amino acids or between atoms,

01:43:55.269 --> 01:43:58.289
and looking at atomic
bonds like a guitar string

01:43:58.289 --> 01:44:00.881
that we can excite, and
watch, and hear, and use

01:44:00.881 --> 01:44:03.110
as a way of creating
a new scaling system

01:44:03.110 --> 01:44:04.550
like the amino acid scale.

01:44:04.550 --> 01:44:07.439
But in all of this process,
even though computers and AI

01:44:07.439 --> 01:44:10.809
can help us, we still need a
significant input, of course,

01:44:10.809 --> 01:44:13.150
and artistic choices
by the human,

01:44:13.150 --> 01:44:16.210
especially once we want to
create artistically interesting

01:44:16.210 --> 01:44:19.719
or aesthetically pleasing
products that we can process

01:44:19.719 --> 01:44:21.559
and understand in our brains.

01:44:21.559 --> 01:44:23.119
So the creation
of music is really

01:44:23.119 --> 01:44:25.320
a deeply human experience.

01:44:25.320 --> 01:44:29.309
It's a way of mapping
out memory of deep time.

01:44:29.309 --> 01:44:31.079
And the individual
and collective process

01:44:31.079 --> 01:44:34.659
by which music is
created, performed,

01:44:34.659 --> 01:44:38.249
improvised, exposed
on, analyzed on,

01:44:38.249 --> 01:44:40.179
derived for future
evolutions of musical--

01:44:40.179 --> 01:44:41.809
cultural and musical creation.

01:44:41.809 --> 01:44:45.519
It's really an
unconscious act to create

01:44:45.519 --> 01:44:46.880
consciousness of the past.

01:44:46.880 --> 01:44:51.210
And this concept is
artistically very interesting.

01:44:51.210 --> 01:44:53.420
It's also scientifically
very interesting.

01:44:53.420 --> 01:44:56.000
Because using music as a way
of looking inside ourselves,

01:44:56.000 --> 01:44:59.059
to looking at these
distant primordial memories

01:44:59.059 --> 01:45:01.090
or structures that
might have happened

01:45:01.090 --> 01:45:03.420
during the emergence of
life billions of years

01:45:03.420 --> 01:45:07.619
ago, here on this planet,
perhaps even further, deeper

01:45:07.619 --> 01:45:10.120
in time, during the emergence of
the universe as a whole, where

01:45:10.120 --> 01:45:12.489
quantum fluctuations
have emerged.

01:45:12.489 --> 01:45:14.459
And I think this is a
very interesting direction

01:45:14.459 --> 01:45:16.610
for research in my lab

01:45:16.610 --> 01:45:19.590
to explore whether
these relationships can

01:45:19.590 --> 01:45:20.860
be brought out.

01:45:20.860 --> 01:45:22.630
Whether we can
make them audible.

01:45:22.630 --> 01:45:25.580
Whether we can connect them
mathematically using concepts

01:45:25.580 --> 01:45:28.079
like category theory
to other principles

01:45:28.079 --> 01:45:30.190
and organization of
information, and thereby

01:45:30.190 --> 01:45:33.300
solve of course, ultimately,
an important cultural problem

01:45:33.300 --> 01:45:36.969
or scientific problem, or to
advance the ability of humans

01:45:36.969 --> 01:45:40.769
to create music and to create
this beautiful abstract form

01:45:40.769 --> 01:45:45.489
of art that allows us to
transmit information, emotions,

01:45:45.489 --> 01:45:48.030
without ever using
a single word.

01:45:48.030 --> 01:45:50.079
So the opportunities
ahead are many fold.

01:45:50.079 --> 01:45:53.820
We can see that by looking at
multiple scales in a material

01:45:53.820 --> 01:45:57.130
you can watch individual
molecules move.

01:45:57.130 --> 01:45:59.449
You can watch
spiders build webs.

01:45:59.449 --> 01:46:01.849
We can make spider
webs into instruments.

01:46:01.849 --> 01:46:03.550
We can make molecules
into instruments.

01:46:03.550 --> 01:46:04.699
We can hear music
everywhere, really.

01:46:04.699 --> 01:46:06.650
We can hear the
sound everywhere.

01:46:06.650 --> 01:46:10.300
And this tradition, of
course, of using sonification,

01:46:10.300 --> 01:46:13.260
or the tradition of
listening to the environment

01:46:13.260 --> 01:46:17.699
or understanding how silence,
or the lack of music,

01:46:17.699 --> 01:46:21.030
the expectation,
like in 4'33 yields

01:46:21.030 --> 01:46:24.110
this particular result of paying
close attention to nature,

01:46:24.110 --> 01:46:27.789
close attention to the
environment, is powerful.

01:46:27.789 --> 01:46:30.320
And with the tools of
AI, molecular dynamics,

01:46:30.320 --> 01:46:33.329
multi-scale experimentation,
microscopes, really--

01:46:33.329 --> 01:46:36.030
whether computational
or real microscopes--

01:46:36.030 --> 01:46:38.889
we can look inside
materials and understand

01:46:38.889 --> 01:46:40.590
a lot of these
principles at a very

01:46:40.590 --> 01:46:42.309
deep and foundational level.

01:46:42.309 --> 01:46:44.869
And what you're hearing
now in this last example

01:46:44.869 --> 01:46:47.519
is music created during
the fracturing process

01:46:47.519 --> 01:46:50.650
by analyzing the stress tensor.

01:46:50.650 --> 01:46:52.630
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:46:52.630 --> 01:46:55.289
I'd like to thank you very
much for your interest

01:46:55.289 --> 01:46:56.739
and for your time.

01:46:56.739 --> 01:46:59.460
Please be in touch by email
if you have questions.

01:46:59.460 --> 01:47:00.729
Follow me on my
SoundCloud page, where

01:47:00.729 --> 01:47:05.110
you can hear a lot of examples
of music created from proteins,

01:47:05.110 --> 01:47:08.920
music created from fractures,
and sounds of spider webs.

01:47:08.920 --> 01:47:11.060
And of course, you can
also follow me on Twitter

01:47:11.060 --> 01:47:13.090
where I post information
about my latest

01:47:13.090 --> 01:47:15.789
research on a regular basis.

01:47:15.789 --> 01:47:17.500
Thank you very much, and I'll
see you, hopefully, soon,

01:47:17.500 --> 01:47:17.929
in the panel.

